          answer: 'What happens in the retina: in bright light the rods, which are the receptors responsible for vision in dim light, have had their pigment rhodopsin bleached — broken down by the light faster than it can be reformed — so almost none is available. Cones function in bright light but need far more light than the storeroom provides, so at first the learner sees almost nothing. In the dark, rhodopsin is gradually resynthesised from its components, the rods become sensitive again, and vision in dim light returns over several minutes. The pupil also dilates, as the radial muscles of the iris contract, letting in more light, but that happens in seconds and is the smaller part of the effect. Evaluating the claim: it is partly true and the conclusion does not follow. The true part is that rhodopsin is made from a derivative of vitamin A, and carrots are rich in beta-carotene, which the body converts to vitamin A. A person who is genuinely deficient in vitamin A cannot make enough rhodopsin and does suffer night blindness, and for them more vitamin A really does improve vision in dim light. The part that does not follow is the advice to the learner. Someone whose vitamin A intake is already adequate gains nothing from eating more: the rate of rhodopsin resynthesis is not limited by the supply of vitamin A once that supply is sufficient, so dark adaptation will take the same several minutes however many carrots are eaten. Correcting a deficiency restores normal function; exceeding a requirement does not produce better than normal function, and very large intakes of vitamin A are in fact toxic.',
