          prompt:
            'Describe an experiment that could be used to demonstrate that a chemical substance (rather than light itself) causes phototropism in a growing plant shoot.',
          answer:
            'Take several identical growing seedlings and expose them all to light from one direction. Cover the tip of one seedling with an opaque cap (blocking light from reaching the tip specifically), leave a second seedling with its tip covered by a transparent cap (allowing light through), and leave a third as an uncovered control. After a period of growth, only the seedlings whose tips were exposed to light (the transparent-capped and uncovered seedlings) will bend toward the light, while the opaque-capped seedling will grow straight up. This shows that the shoot tip specifically must detect the light (not the rest of the shoot), and further experiments (inserting an impermeable barrier just below the tip, which also prevents bending even with the tip exposed to light) show that a chemical produced at the tip must move down the shoot to cause the bending, rather than light acting directly on the bending region itself.',
          explanation: 'This classic type of experiment (based on the work of Charles and Francis Darwin, and later Boysen-Jensen) isolates the role of the shoot tip in detecting light from the actual bending response lower down the shoot, providing strong evidence that a mobile chemical messenger (later identified as auxin), rather than light acting directly, causes the curvature.',
