          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'A hiker is found after a night out in cold, wet conditions. She is shivering violently and her core temperature is low. One rescuer wants to rub her arms and legs vigorously to "get the blood moving" and give her brandy to warm her up. Another says to remove the wet clothing, wrap her in dry insulation, and warm the trunk of the body first.',
          prompt: 'Explain the thermoregulatory responses the hiker is showing, and decide which rescuer gives the better advice. Justify your decision.',
          answer: 'What her body is doing: the hypothalamus has detected a core temperature below the set point, from thermoreceptors in the skin and from the temperature of the blood reaching it. It is triggering the heat-conserving and heat-generating responses. Vasoconstriction of the skin arterioles reduces blood flow to the surface, so less heat is lost by radiation and the skin becomes pale and cold. Shivering — rapid involuntary contraction of skeletal muscle — releases heat from respiration. Hair erector muscles contract, and metabolic rate is raised by thyroxine and adrenaline. The wet clothing is working against all of this, because evaporation from wet fabric removes heat continuously. The second rescuer is right. Rubbing the limbs vigorously forces the vasoconstriction open and sends blood that has been sitting cold in the arms and legs back to the core, which LOWERS the core temperature further at the worst possible moment. Brandy is worse: alcohol causes vasodilation of the skin arterioles, which produces the familiar feeling of warmth precisely because warm blood is being brought to the surface — where the heat is then lost. It makes the person feel warmer while cooling them faster, and it also depresses shivering. Removing the wet clothing stops the evaporative loss, dry insulation reduces further loss, and warming the trunk raises the core without opening the peripheral circulation. The judgement rests on one point: in hypothermia the body has deliberately shut the periphery down, and every part of the first rescuer’s advice reopens it.',
          explanation: 'The first rescuer’s instincts are the ordinary ones — rubbing and brandy both produce a sensation of warmth — and that is exactly why the physiology has to decide it. The general principle is worth carrying: vasoconstriction in the cold is not a problem to be overcome but the body protecting the core at the expense of the limbs, and an intervention that reverses a protective response is harmful however good it feels.',
