          answer: 'One mechanism accounts for both: natural selection by bird predation, acting on camouflage against the tree trunks. The dark allele was already present in the population before 1848, kept rare because dark moths stood out against pale, lichen-covered bark and were eaten. As soot blackened the trunks and killed the lichen, the advantage reversed — dark moths were now camouflaged and pale moths conspicuous — so dark moths survived and bred more successfully and their frequency climbed to about 98%. After the Clean Air Acts the bark became pale again and the lichen returned, so the advantage reversed a second time, pale moths were once more the better camouflaged, and the dark frequency fell back to about 10%. The environment changed; the mechanism did not. The fall rules out the idea that the pollution CAUSED the dark colouring — that soot somehow darkened the moths or induced a mutation for darkness. If pollution had produced the dark form, removing the pollution could not have restored pale moths to dominance so quickly, because the population would have to wait for new pale variation to arise. The rapid fall shows that pale alleles were present throughout, even at the peak, and that pollution only ever altered WHICH pre-existing variant was favoured.',
          explanation: 'The reversal is what makes this data set worth more than the familiar rise alone, and it does two jobs. It shows selection has no direction of its own — it tracks the environment, and will run a change backwards as readily as forwards. And it refutes the most common misconception about this example, that the environment produces the variation it selects. Variation arises first, independently of need; the environment only sorts it. Note also that the dark form never reached 100% and the pale form was never lost, which is why the recovery could be so fast.',
