          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'The Galápagos Islands hold many finch species. Each has a beak suited to a particular food: deep, heavy beaks on islands where large hard seeds dominate; fine, pointed beaks where insects are the main food; a long straight beak in a species that uses a cactus spine to winkle grubs out of bark. Their DNA sequences are all extremely similar to one another, and all are most similar to a single finch species on the South American mainland.',
          prompt: 'A learner says the beak shapes alone prove that all the finches descend from one mainland ancestor. Evaluate that claim, explain the process that produced the diversity of beaks, and state what the DNA evidence establishes that the beaks could not.',
          answer: 'The claim goes further than the beaks can carry it. The process that produced the diversity: a small founding population reached the islands and spread across them. The islands differ in what food is available, and the population on each was subject to its own selection pressure. Variation in beak depth and shape already existed; on an island of large hard seeds the birds with deeper, heavier beaks fed more successfully, survived and left more offspring, while on an island where insects dominated the finer, pointed beaks were favoured. Because beak shape is heritable, each island population shifted towards the form that suited it. Separated by water, the populations could not exchange genes, so they diverged and eventually became reproductively isolated — allopatric speciation, producing an adaptive radiation from one ancestral form. Why the beaks alone do not prove it: the beaks show that each species suits its food, which is evidence about SELECTION, not about ancestry. A beak matching its food source is exactly what convergent evolution would also produce if several unrelated finch-like birds had arrived separately and each adapted to a different island. Judged on beaks alone, a deep-beaked finch resembles other deep-beaked birds elsewhere more than it resembles its own island’s insect-eater, which is precisely the misleading signal the marsupials and the wolf produce. What the DNA adds: the sequences of all the Galápagos species are extremely similar to one another, and all are most similar to a single mainland species. That is evidence of ancestry rather than of adaptation, because DNA sequence is inherited and is largely independent of what a bird eats. It establishes the single common ancestor and the single colonisation that the beaks could only suggest — and it does so precisely because it is not the thing selection was shaping.',
          explanation: 'The same rule appears here for the third time in this section and is worth stating in general: evidence about how well a structure fits its job tells you about selection, and evidence about features the job does not dictate tells you about ancestry. DNA is the cleanest case of the second kind, which is why it settled a question Darwin could only argue. The learner is not wrong about the conclusion, only about which evidence supports it — and that distinction is what the marks are for.',
