          grade: 12,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'Three documented cases. (a) A hospital bacterial population goes from almost no antibiotic resistance to almost complete resistance over three years. (b) The peppered moth population shifts from about 2% dark to about 98% dark over roughly fifty years, then back again. (c) Whales evolve from four-legged land mammals over roughly fifty million years, by way of fossil forms with progressively reduced hind limbs.',
          prompt: 'A learner says that macroevolution "must involve some extra process, because you cannot get whales from land mammals just by the kind of change that turns moths dark". Evaluate the learner’s claim, classifying each of the three cases and giving your reason.',
          answer: 'The claim is not supported, and classifying the cases shows why. Case (a), antibiotic resistance rising from almost none to almost complete in three years, is MICROEVOLUTION: a change in allele frequency within a species, with no new species formed. Case (b), the peppered moth going from 2% to 98% dark and back again, is also microevolution, for the same reason — the population is the same species throughout, and the frequency of an existing allele has shifted with the environment. Case (c), whales evolving from four-legged land mammals over fifty million years by way of forms with progressively reduced hind limbs, is MACROEVOLUTION: change above the species level, producing new species, genera and families. So the classification turns on the SCALE and the outcome, not on the mechanism. Why the learner is wrong about the mechanism: the same processes operate in every case — variation arising by mutation and recombination, differential survival and reproduction, and the consequent change in allele frequencies, together with the reproductive isolation that allows diverged populations to become separate species. What differs is the amount of time and the accumulation. Case (c) is not one event but tens of millions of years of cases like (a) and (b), one after another, with isolation allowing the changes to accumulate separately. The fossil series with progressively reduced hind limbs is the direct evidence for that: it shows the transition happening in steps, not in a leap that would need explaining. The learner’s intuition is really about magnitude — the whale outcome feels too large for the moth mechanism — and the answer is that fifty million years is an extremely large number of moth-sized steps. No additional process has ever been needed or found.',
