          answer: 'Gravity causes auxin to accumulate on the lower side of both the shoot and the root. In the shoot a higher auxin concentration PROMOTES cell elongation, so the lower-side cells elongate more than the upper-side cells and the shoot curves upward, away from gravity — negative geotropism. In the root the same accumulation on the lower side has the opposite effect, because roots are inhibited by the concentrations that stimulate shoots: the lower-side cells elongate less than the upper-side cells, so the root curves downward, towards gravity — positive geotropism. One redistribution of one hormone therefore bends the two organs in opposite directions.',
