          marks: 6,
          context: 'A runner plans to rehydrate after a long training session with several strong cups of coffee. A friend tells her this will make her MORE dehydrated, because "coffee is a diuretic, so every cup takes out more water than it puts in".',
          prompt: 'Explain how ADH and the kidneys regulate water balance, and evaluate the friend’s claim, deciding whether coffee would leave the runner with a net water loss.',
          answer: 'How the regulation works: osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect the concentration of the blood. After a long training session the runner has lost water in sweat, so her blood is more concentrated and its volume lower. The posterior pituitary therefore releases more ADH, which increases the permeability of the collecting ducts of the nephrons to water. More water is reabsorbed from the filtrate into the blood, and a small volume of concentrated, dark urine is produced. As she drinks and the blood is diluted back towards normal, ADH release falls again and a larger volume of paler urine is produced. Evaluating the claim: the friend is right about the mechanism and wrong about the arithmetic. Caffeine does have a mild diuretic effect, partly by increasing blood flow through the kidney and partly by opposing the action of ADH, so a given cup of coffee produces somewhat more urine than the same volume of water would. But the cup is mostly water, and the extra urine produced is a fraction of the volume drunk, not more than it. The net effect is still a gain of water. A drink would have to cause the body to excrete more water than the drink contains before it could dehydrate anyone, and coffee at ordinary strength does not come close. The effect is also smaller in habitual coffee drinkers, who develop tolerance to it. So the runner will rehydrate, just slightly less efficiently than with water. The fair qualification is that coffee replaces no sodium, and after a long session in the heat the salts lost in sweat matter as well as the water.',
