          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'Early embryos of a fish, a chicken and a human all develop a series of pharyngeal arches — ridges of tissue in the neck region, separated by grooves. In the fish these arches go on to form the gill supports. In the human, the first arch forms jaw structures and contributes the tiny bones of the middle ear, and the grooves close over; no gill ever forms.',
          prompt: 'A learner argues that pharyngeal arches in a human embryo are simply "the efficient way to build a neck", not evidence of ancestry. Evaluate that argument, and explain why a human embryo forming arches that never become gills is more informative than if the human embryo simply skipped that stage.',
          answer: 'The argument does not hold, and the reason is that the arches are not what building a human neck requires. If they were merely an efficient route to the adult structure, we would expect the developmental path to be shaped by the destination. It is not. The human embryo builds a series of arches separated by grooves — the same series, in the same order, that a fish embryo builds — and then repurposes them: the first forms jaw structures and contributes the tiny bones of the middle ear, and the grooves close over without any gill ever forming. Building a set of arches and grooves in order to close the grooves again and convert the arches into ear ossicles is a detour, not an efficiency. What it indicates: the human, the chicken and the fish share the developmental programme because they inherited it from a common ancestor in which the arches did form gill supports. Later modifications redirect the material, but the early programme itself is conserved, because so much is built on top of it that altering it disturbs everything downstream. Why this is more informative than skipping the stage: if the human embryo went straight to jaw and ear, the observation would be consistent with evolution but would say nothing, since a separately created human would look the same. What has to be explained is the presence of a structure with no function in the organism that builds it and a clear function in a relative. Common ancestry explains it; efficient design does not, because no efficiency is gained. That is the general form of the strongest evidence in this topic — the thing that makes no sense as design and complete sense as history — and it is the same argument the embryonic tail and the coccyx make.',
          explanation: 'The learner is applying a reasonable-sounding test, that a feature must be there because it is useful, and the question is whether the feature passes it. Asking what the arches contribute to building a human neck is the move that settles it: closing a groove you just made is not a saving. It is worth noticing how often the good evidence in evolution has this shape, of a cost or a detour rather than a neat fit.',
