          grade: 12,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'Case 1: the Hawaiian honeycreepers, about fifty bird species found only on that archipelago, with beaks ranging from short and thick to long and curved, feeding on seeds, insects and nectar. Their DNA shows they are all each other’s closest relatives. Case 2: the dolphin (a mammal), the ichthyosaur (an extinct reptile) and the shark (a fish) all have a streamlined body, a dorsal fin and a tail fin. Their DNA and skeletons place them in three distantly related groups.',
          prompt: 'A learner says that the honeycreepers and the dolphin-ichthyosaur-shark group illustrate the same thing, "because in both cases the animals came to suit their way of life". Evaluate that claim, identify the process each case illustrates, and state what feature of the EVIDENCE — not of the animals’ appearance — allows the two to be told apart.',
          answer: 'The claim identifies something true and misses the distinction that matters. It is true that in both cases the animals came to suit their way of life, because that is what natural selection does everywhere. What differs is where the lineages STARTED, and that is a different question from what they ended up suiting. Case 1, the Hawaiian honeycreepers: ADAPTIVE RADIATION, a form of divergent evolution. One ancestral species reached the archipelago and its descendants spread into different ways of life — seeds, insects, nectar — diverging in beak shape as each population was selected for a different food. Case 2, the dolphin, the ichthyosaur and the shark: CONVERGENT EVOLUTION. Three distantly related lineages — a mammal, a reptile and a fish — independently arrived at a streamlined body, a dorsal fin and a tail fin, because fast swimming in water imposes the same physical demands whatever you start from. The feature of the evidence that tells them apart is the DNA and skeletal relationships, not the outward form. For the honeycreepers, the DNA shows all fifty species to be each other’s closest relatives, so their differences in appearance are divergence from one recent ancestor. For the dolphin group, the DNA and skeletons place the three in distantly related groups, so their similarity in appearance cannot be inherited and must have arisen separately. Outward appearance is useless here, and misleading in both directions at once: it makes the honeycreepers look like fifty unrelated kinds of bird when they are close relatives, and makes the dolphin and the shark look like relatives when they are not. Appearance reports the way of life, which is what selection shaped; ancestry has to be read from what selection was not shaping.',
          explanation: 'The learner has found a genuine common factor and treated it as a reason the two cases are the same, which is worth separating out — everything alive suits its way of life, so that observation cannot distinguish anything. The distinction is about starting point: one ancestor becoming many forms, or many ancestors becoming one form. And the rule for telling them apart is the one this section keeps returning to: read ancestry from the features the job does not dictate.',
