          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'The Kaibab squirrel lives only on the north rim of the Grand Canyon and the closely related Abert squirrel on the south rim. The canyon separated them and they no longer interbreed across it. The Kaibab has a white tail and a dark belly; the Abert has a grey tail and a white belly. Biologists nonetheless classify the Kaibab squirrel as a SUBSPECIES of the Abert squirrel, not as a separate species.',
          prompt: 'A visitor argues that the two squirrels must already be separate species, "because they look different and never interbreed". Evaluate that argument, explain how the canyon has affected the two populations, and state what evidence would be needed before biologists would reclassify them.',
          answer: 'The argument fails, because neither piece of evidence it rests on establishes what it claims. Looking different is not the test: variation in colour and markings occurs within a single species, and human populations differ visibly without being separate species. Never interbreeding is closer to the point but is still not sufficient, because these two populations never MEET. The canyon keeps them apart geographically, so the absence of interbreeding is explained entirely by the barrier and tells us nothing about whether they still COULD interbreed. That distinction — being separated and being incapable — is exactly what the classification turns on. What the canyon has done: it divided an originally single population into two that can no longer exchange genes. Each has since been subject to its own mutations, its own genetic drift and the selection pressures of its own rim, so the two have diverged in appearance. That is allopatric speciation IN PROGRESS. Why they are still subspecies: enough divergence to be recognisable is not the same as reproductive isolation. Biologists classify them as subspecies because the divergence so far is in appearance rather than in the ability to breed. What evidence would be needed: that the two populations, when brought together and given the opportunity, either do not mate or produce no offspring, or produce offspring that are infertile. Either outcome would show reproductive isolation rather than mere geographic separation, and that is the criterion for separate species. Until such a test is done, the honest classification is the more conservative one.',
          explanation: 'The species question is a good one for judging evidence, because the observations that feel most convincing — they look different, they never mix — are the two that do not settle it. The reason a subspecies is the cautious answer is that it is a claim the evidence supports, while "separate species" is a claim about what WOULD happen in a situation nobody has created. Naming the test that would settle it is what turns an opinion into a judgement.',
