          answer: 'External fertilisation persists because it works well enough in the conditions frogs breed in, and because what matters is whether enough offspring survive, not how many are lost. Releasing gametes into water is only possible at all because water stops them drying out and lets the sperm swim to the eggs, and it costs the frog very little: no internal development, no shell, no care, so the female can convert most of her reproductive investment directly into egg number. Selection has no way to replace it with something better simply because it is wasteful — it can only favour what raises surviving offspring, and in a wet year this strategy produces a great many. The 3 000 eggs are the answer to the risks rather than a separate fact about the frog. Each individual risk — sperm failing to reach an egg, the egg being eaten, the pond drying — removes a large fraction of the clutch, so the only way to end a season with survivors is to start with a number large enough that a small surviving fraction is still enough to replace the parents. Producing them all at once also means some tadpoles complete development before the pond dries in a bad year, and a predator that finds the spawn can only eat so much of it at one time.',
