          context: 'A dancer spins rapidly and stops. For a few seconds the room seems to keep turning, although she is standing still. A learner watching says this shows that "the semicircular canals are unreliable, so balance must really come from the eyes".',
          prompt: 'Explain what produces the sensation of continued turning, and evaluate the learner’s conclusion about where balance comes from.',
          answer: 'What produces the sensation: the three semicircular canals are filled with fluid and contain sensory hair cells in a swelling at the base of each. When the head begins to rotate, the fluid lags behind because of its inertia, bending the hair cells, and the impulses generated are sent to the cerebellum, which is interpreted as turning. During a sustained spin the fluid catches up and moves with the canal, so the hair cells return to rest. When the dancer stops, the fluid keeps moving for a few seconds — inertia again — and bends the hair cells in the OPPOSITE direction, so the brain receives impulses reporting a rotation that is not happening. The eyes, meanwhile, correctly report a stationary room. The conflict between the two accounts is what produces the dizziness and the sense that the room is spinning. Evaluating the conclusion: it is wrong, although it starts from something real. The canals are not unreliable; they are doing precisely what they are built to do, which is to detect CHANGES in rotation. The dancer has created an artificial situation the system did not evolve for, and the after-effect is the predictable consequence of fluid inertia, not a defect. Nor does balance come from the eyes alone. It comes from three sources integrated by the cerebellum: the vestibular apparatus, vision, and proprioceptors in the muscles and joints reporting the position of the body. That is why a person can stand with their eyes closed, and why someone with inner-ear damage becomes markedly unsteady in the dark, when vision can no longer compensate. Relying on any one of the three would be far less robust than combining them, and the dizziness the learner points to is evidence of the integration rather than against it.',
