          prompt: 'A potted seedling on a windowsill bends towards the window over several days. The pot is then turned through 180°. Explain, using auxin, why the shoot bent towards the window, and predict what the shoot will do over the days that follow.',
          answer: 'Auxin is made at the shoot tip and moves down the shoot. Light from the window causes auxin to move laterally to the shaded side, so auxin accumulates there. Auxin promotes cell elongation in shoots, so cells on the shaded side elongate more than those on the lit side and the unequal growth bends the shoot towards the light. Once the pot is turned, the side that was shaded faces the window and the previously lit side is now in shade. Auxin again accumulates on whichever side is darker, so elongation is now greatest on the opposite side and the shoot will bend back the other way, growing towards the window once more over the following days.',
