          prompt: 'A learner says the test cross was unnecessary, because "the tall parent could just have been crossed with another tall plant, and short offspring would show it was heterozygous". Evaluate the learner’s suggestion, and determine the genotype of the unknown tall parent from the result actually obtained.',
          answer: 'The genotype first. Tall is dominant, so the unknown tall plant is either TT or Tt. If it were TT, every gamete would carry T, and crossing with tt would give Tt offspring only — all tall. The observed result is half tall and half short, so it cannot be TT. If it is Tt, half its gametes carry T and half carry t; crossing with tt, which produces only t gametes, gives 50% Tt (tall) and 50% tt (short), which matches. The unknown parent is therefore heterozygous, Tt. Evaluating the suggestion: the learner’s cross does give useful information, but it is a WORSE test, for one specific reason. Crossing Tt with Tt gives 3 tall : 1 short, so short offspring do indeed reveal the heterozygote — when they appear. The probability that any single offspring is short is only 1 in 4, so a heterozygote can easily produce a run of tall offspring by chance and be mistaken for a homozygote; with four offspring the chance of seeing no short plant at all is about 32%. The test cross with tt gives 1 tall : 1 short, so half of all offspring are informative rather than a quarter, and a single short offspring settles it with far fewer plants. There is also a second problem the learner has not seen: if the other tall plant is of unknown genotype too, then an all-tall result tells you nothing about EITHER parent. Crossing with a known homozygous recessive is what makes the result readable, because that parent contributes a known allele every time and so the offspring phenotype reports the unknown parent’s gamete directly. Both crosses can work; the test cross is the one designed so that a negative result means something.',
          explanation: 'This is about experimental design rather than about Mendel, which is why it is worth asking. The reason the test cross is standard is not that the other cross is invalid but that it wastes offspring and can mislead, and being able to say WHY — informative offspring per cross, and a known allele from one parent — is the difference between reciting a technique and understanding it. The 32% figure is worth carrying: absence of evidence in a small sample is weak evidence of absence.',
