          prompt: 'A learner looks at a roan calf and concludes that this must be incomplete dominance, "because the calf is a blend of the two parents, exactly like pink flowers from red and white". Evaluate the learner’s conclusion using the appearance of the coat, and predict the genotypic and phenotypic ratios if two roan cattle are crossed.',
          answer: 'The conclusion is wrong, and the evidence that refutes it is visible under close examination of the coat. In incomplete dominance the heterozygote has a phenotype genuinely INTERMEDIATE between the two homozygotes: a pink flower is pink in every part, and neither red nor white appears anywhere. A roan coat is not like that. It is not a uniform pink but a mixture of individual red hairs and individual white hairs growing side by side, and a close look finds both parental colours fully expressed. That is codominance: both alleles are expressed fully and separately in the heterozygote, rather than blended. The learner has judged from a distance, where the mixture averages out to a pinkish impression, and the whole distinction lies in whether the intermediate appearance survives closer inspection. Ratios from roan × roan, writing the alleles C^R and C^W: each roan is C^R C^W, so the cross gives 1 C^R C^R : 2 C^R C^W : 1 C^W C^W. Genotypic ratio 1 : 2 : 1, and phenotypic ratio 1 red : 2 roan : 1 white. Note that the phenotypic ratio is the SAME as it would be under incomplete dominance, which is the second half of the point: a ratio alone cannot distinguish the two, because in both cases the heterozygote is distinguishable from both homozygotes. Only the nature of the heterozygote’s appearance — mixed versus intermediate — separates them, which is why the question turns on what the coat actually looks like rather than on counting offspring.',
          explanation: 'Two mechanisms that produce identical numbers are a good test of whether a learner is reasoning from evidence or from a memorised ratio. Since 1:2:1 appears either way, the observation that settles it has to be qualitative: are both parental phenotypes present in the heterozygote, or is there a third phenotype that is neither? Human blood group AB is the same situation — both A and B antigens are actually made, rather than some intermediate antigen.',
