          prompt: 'Explain the role of the thalamus, and evaluate the junior doctor’s conclusion, deciding what the test results actually rule in and out.',
          answer: 'The role of the thalamus: it is the relay and filtering station for sensory information. Almost all sensory input — touch, pain, temperature, vision, hearing; smell is the exception — passes through the thalamus on its way to the cortex. It does not simply forward the traffic: it sorts it, directs each type to the correct area of the cortex, and regulates how much of each signal is passed on, which is part of how attention works and why a constant sensation such as clothing on the skin fades from awareness. Evaluating the conclusion: it does not follow, and the error is in the reasoning about pathways rather than in the test results. The tests establish that the two ENDS of the pathway are intact, and say nothing about what lies between them. A pathway is only as good as its weakest link, and the thalamus sits in the middle of this one: damage there means the signals arrive from the skin correctly and the cortex is capable of interpreting them, but too little is relayed across for the patient to feel normally. Faint sensation on one side is exactly what that predicts, since the relay for each side of the body is on the opposite side of the thalamus. So a physical cause has not been ruled out at all; it has simply not been looked for in the right place. The junior doctor has made the reasoning error of treating "the parts I tested are fine" as "no part is at fault", which is only valid if the tested parts are the whole system. The appropriate next step is imaging of the brain to look at the thalamus and the pathways through it, not a psychological explanation by elimination.',
