          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'A seedling on a windowsill bends towards the window, so a learner decides to turn the pot a quarter turn every day. Explain, using auxin, why the shoot bent towards the window, and predict how the plant will grow under the daily rotation.',
          answer: 'Auxin made at the shoot tip moves laterally away from the light and accumulates on the shaded side. Auxin promotes cell elongation in shoots, so the shaded-side cells elongate more than the lit-side cells and the unequal growth bends the shoot towards the window. Turning the pot a quarter turn each day changes which side is shaded before a large bend can build up, so auxin accumulates on a different side each day and the extra elongation is shared around the stem instead of being concentrated on one side. The shoot will therefore grow more or less straight and upright rather than leaning towards the window, though regular turning may leave a slight spiral in the stem.',
          explanation: 'Auxin moves AWAY from light and promotes elongation in shoots, so a bend records where the light has been. The prediction mark is for reasoning about a repeatedly changing stimulus: rotating the pot does not switch the response off, it spreads it evenly, which is why the practice keeps pot plants symmetrical.',
