          marks: 6,
          context: 'Percentage of DNA sequence shared with humans: chimpanzee 98.8%, gorilla 98.4%, orangutan 96.9%, rhesus monkey 93.5%, mouse 85%.',
          prompt: 'A learner reads these figures and concludes that "humans are the most evolved of the five, and the mouse is the least evolved". Evaluate that conclusion, then use the figures to place the five species in order of how recently each shared a common ancestor with humans.',
          answer: 'The conclusion is wrong, and it misreads what the figures measure. There is no such thing as "most evolved". Every one of these five species has been evolving for exactly the same length of time, from the same origin of life, and each is the current end of its own unbroken lineage. A mouse is not an earlier draft of a human; it is a mouse lineage that has been adapting to being a mouse for precisely as long as ours has been adapting to being us. What the percentages measure is SIMILARITY of DNA sequence, which reflects how long ago two lineages separated and have been accumulating independent changes — not progress along a scale, and not complexity. Ordering the species: the higher the percentage shared with humans, the less time there has been for independent mutations to accumulate since the split, so the more recently the common ancestor lived. Most recent first: chimpanzee (98.8%), then gorilla (98.4%), then orangutan (96.9%), then rhesus monkey (93.5%), then mouse (85%). Note that the same figures would be produced if the comparison were run from the mouse’s point of view: the mouse shares 85% with us and we share 85% with the mouse, which is what shows the measure is of DISTANCE between two lineages rather than of rank. The learner’s error also has a shape worth naming: treating the species we happen to be measuring FROM as the top of a ladder. A chart of similarity to the mouse would put the mouse at the top and humans near the bottom, and would mean exactly as much.',
          explanation: 'The ladder picture of evolution is persistent, and DNA percentages are unusually good at exposing it because the measure is symmetrical. If a figure means the same read in either direction it cannot be measuring progress. The ordering itself is straightforward once that is clear, and it is worth noticing that this is a molecular clock argument: change accumulates roughly steadily, so similarity reads as time.',
