          marks: 6,
          context: 'A particular allele is present at a frequency of 1% in a large mainland bird population and confers no advantage or disadvantage. A storm blows 20 birds to an empty island, where they establish a population. One of the 20 carries a single copy of the allele. Fifty generations later the island population numbers several thousand and the allele is present at 30%.',
          prompt: 'Calculate the allele’s frequency among the 20 founding birds, and decide whether natural selection or genetic drift better explains the rise to 30%. Justify your decision, and explain what the size of the founding group has to do with it.',
          answer: 'Each bird is diploid and carries two copies of the gene, so the 20 founders hold 40 copies between them. One copy is the allele in question, so its frequency among the founders is 1 in 40, which is 2.5%. That is already more than double its 1% mainland frequency, and nothing selected for it — the single carrier simply happened to be among the 20 birds the storm blew out to sea. Genetic drift is the better explanation for the rise to 30%. The allele is stated to confer no advantage or disadvantage, so natural selection has nothing to act on: selection changes allele frequencies through differences in survival and reproduction, and where there is no such difference it cannot drive a change. What remains is chance — which individuals happen to breed, and how many offspring each happens to leave — and across fifty generations those chance effects accumulated in one direction. This is the founder effect, a form of genetic drift, and the size of the founding group is the whole of why it worked. In a population of 20, one carrier is 2.5% of the gene pool, whereas in a population of many thousands that same single carrier would be a vanishingly small fraction and any chance fluctuation would be swamped by the sheer number of other individuals. Drift is powerful in small populations and weak in large ones, which is why a founding event is where it shows most strongly.',
          explanation: 'Do the 1-in-40 calculation first: it shows the frequency had already jumped before a single island generation passed, which is what makes the founder event itself, rather than fifty generations of anything, the start of the story. The decisive clue for the second part is the statement that the allele is neutral — that removes selection as a candidate by definition, since selection acts only on differences in survival and reproduction. The link to population size is the general principle worth carrying away: chance events average out in large samples and do not in small ones, which is the whole reason small populations drift.',
