          prompt: 'A conservation manager argues that this population "has fully recovered, since the numbers are back to what they were, so no further intervention is needed". Evaluate that argument in terms of the bottleneck effect.',
          answer: 'The argument is wrong, because numbers and genetic diversity are two different things and only one of them has recovered. What the data show: the drought killed 95% of the population in a short time, and which individuals survived was largely a matter of chance rather than of which alleles they carried. The survivors therefore carried only a small, arbitrary SAMPLE of the alleles present before — that is the bottleneck effect. Every individual in the recovered population is descended from that small group, so the population can only contain the alleles the survivors happened to have. Breeding back up to full numbers multiplies copies of those alleles; it cannot restore the ones that were lost, which is exactly why the measured diversity stays low long after the count is back, and why it is still low generations later. Why this matters for the decision: genetic diversity is the raw material natural selection acts on. A population with little of it has little for selection to work with if conditions change again — a new disease, another drought, a new predator — because the variant that would have coped may simply no longer exist, and selection cannot create one to order. Low diversity also means more mating between relatives, so harmful recessive alleles more often appear in homozygotes, reducing fertility and survival. So a population at full numbers can be considerably more fragile than an identical count before the bottleneck. The only processes that restore diversity are mutation, which is far too slow to help on a management timescale, and gene flow from another population, which is why introducing individuals from elsewhere is a standard intervention. The manager is monitoring the variable that is easy to count rather than the one that predicts survival.',
          explanation: 'This is a decision that hangs on choosing the right measure, and the easy measure is the misleading one. The underlying point is that a population is not only a number of animals but a store of alleles, and the drought took a permanent bite out of the second while the first regrew. The practical conclusion — that new individuals from another population are worth more than protection alone — follows directly, and is why conservation genetics exists as a field.',
