          marks: 6,
          context: 'Two populations of a bird species live on adjacent hillsides separated by a valley. Over several thousand years they have come to differ in song and in plumage colour, and each population prefers to mate with its own type. However, in every generation about 5% of birds cross the valley and breed successfully with the other population, producing healthy, fertile young.',
          prompt: 'A learner says the two hillside populations "are already separate species, because they look different, sound different and prefer their own kind". Evaluate that claim, and predict whether they are likely to become separate species if this situation continues. Explain what the 5% figure has to do with your prediction.',
          answer: 'The claim is wrong, and the 5% is the reason. Why they are not yet separate species: differing in song and plumage, and preferring to mate with their own type, are real steps towards isolation, but the test is whether gene flow has STOPPED. It has not. In every generation about 5% of birds cross the valley and breed successfully, and the young are healthy and fertile. Fertile hybrids mean alleles from each population enter the other and are passed on, so the two gene pools are still connected. A preference is not a barrier; it only reduces the rate. Prediction: on this evidence they are unlikely to become separate species while the situation continues. Gene flow is the force that opposes divergence — it continually mixes the alleles that mutation, drift and local selection are separating — and even a small amount of it is enough to hold two populations together, because the effect compounds over every generation. Divergence proceeds only when the rate at which differences accumulate exceeds the rate at which interbreeding erases them, and a steady 5% each generation is a great deal of mixing on an evolutionary timescale. What WOULD change the prediction, and this is worth stating: if the valley widened or the crossing became harder so that the 5% fell towards zero; if the mating preferences strengthened until crossers no longer bred; or if hybrids began to be less fit than either parent type, since selection would then favour any trait reducing the chance of cross-mating and would drive the preference further apart. Any of those removes or reverses the gene flow, and the divergence already present could then continue to completion.',
