          marks: 3,
          prompt: 'State what is meant by a common ancestor, and state what a branching point on a phylogenetic tree (evolutionary tree) represents.',
          answer: 'A common ancestor is an ancestral species from which two or more different species have descended (2). A branching point represents the point in time at which one ancestral population split into two lines that then evolved separately (1).',
          explanation: 'Reading a phylogenetic tree correctly depends on this idea: relatedness is measured by how recently two species shared an ancestor, not by how similar they look.',
