          answer: 'The statement is partly right about the shell and wrong about what follows from it. The reptile egg does solve a problem the amphibian egg cannot: laid on dry land, it would lose water rapidly by evaporation, so it has a tough, water-retaining shell, and an internal supply of water and food, which lets it develop away from water altogether. That released reptiles from breeding in ponds and let them colonise dry regions. But the amphibian egg is not a failed version of it, because it is not solving that problem. An amphibian egg is laid in water, where drying out cannot happen, and its jelly coat swells with water, cushions the egg and makes it awkward for a predator to swallow. A shell would in fact be a disadvantage there, since it would add weight and hinder the gas exchange that the jelly allows directly with the surrounding water. So neither egg is more advanced. Each is adapted to where it is laid, and calling one better ignores that they face different conditions. Amphibians are also not less well adapted than reptiles: they have persisted for hundreds of millions of years in the habitats their eggs suit, which is the only measure of adaptation that means anything.',
