          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'A seed germinates near the soil surface where light still reaches it, yet its primary root grows downward. Explain, in terms of auxin, why the root grows downward, and explain why the light does not turn it upward.',
          answer: 'Gravity causes auxin to accumulate on the lower side of the root. In roots, unlike shoots, a higher auxin concentration INHIBITS cell elongation, so the lower-side cells elongate less than the upper-side cells and the root curves downward — positive geotropism. Light has little effect because roots are far less sensitive to it than shoots, and because the gravity response dominates. Where a weak phototropic response does exist in a root it is NEGATIVE, bending the root away from light, which for a seed near the surface also points it downward. The two stimuli therefore push the same way rather than competing — and the root is in any case growing quickly into soil that no light reaches.',
          explanation: 'Auxin inhibits elongation in roots and promotes it in shoots, which is why one accumulation bends them oppositely. The applied mark is for handling two stimuli at once: where responses could conflict, the questions are which dominates and whether they in fact agree.',
