          context: 'A bird wing contains a humerus, a radius and ulna, wrist bones and reduced digits. A bat wing contains the same bones in the same order, with the digits enormously elongated to carry the membrane. A bee wing contains no bones at all: it is a thin membrane of cuticle braced by hollow veins.',
          prompt: 'A learner concludes that the bird and the bee must be closely related, "because their wings are a similar size and do exactly the same job". Evaluate the learner’s conclusion, deciding which pairs of wings are homologous and which are analogous, and giving your reason in each case.',
          answer: 'The conclusion is wrong, and the reason exposes the rule for using this kind of evidence. The bird wing and the bat wing are HOMOLOGOUS. Both contain a humerus, a radius and ulna, wrist bones and digits, in the same order and the same relative positions, even though the bat’s digits are enormously elongated to carry a membrane and the bird’s are reduced. A shared underlying STRUCTURE that the function does not require is evidence of inheritance from a common ancestor that had that structure, and it indicates divergent evolution. The bird wing and the bee wing are ANALOGOUS. The bee wing contains no bones at all: it is a thin membrane of cuticle braced by hollow veins, built on a completely different plan. The two structures share only what they do. Similar function arising in unrelated lineages facing the same problem — staying in the air — is convergent evolution, and it is evidence about the environment rather than about ancestry. Why the learner is wrong: they have used the wrong feature. Function and size are what selection shapes directly, so any two lineages under the same pressure can arrive at the same function from different starting points, which is why analogous structures exist at all. Underlying structure is what is inherited, and a shared structure that the function does not demand is the thing that cannot easily be explained except by descent. The test to apply is therefore not "do they do the same job?" but "is the same plan there beneath the job?" — and beneath the bee wing there is no plan in common at all.',
          explanation: 'This is the single most useful distinction in the evidence for evolution, and the learner has made the error it exists to prevent. Notice why the homologous case is the persuasive one: the bat’s and the bird’s bone arrangements are not what flight REQUIRES — the bee proves that, since it flies well with no bones — so their shared arrangement needs some explanation other than the job, and inheritance is the available one.',
