          topicId: 'life-sci-response-humans',
          grade: 12,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Explain the role of the sino-atrial node (SAN) in controlling heartbeat.',
          answer: 'The sino-atrial node (SAN), located in the wall of the right atrium, generates a regular electrical impulse that spreads across both atria, causing them to contract; it acts as the heart’s natural "pacemaker", setting the basic rhythm and rate of the heartbeat without requiring any external nervous signal to initiate each individual beat.',
          explanation: 'Because the SAN can generate its own rhythmic electrical signal independently, the heart continues to beat in a coordinated, regular rhythm even if disconnected from the nervous system (as can be demonstrated by a heart continuing to beat outside the body for a short time), though the nervous system can still speed up or slow down the SAN’s own natural rate.',
