          marks: 5,
          context: 'A bacterium in a warm wound divides roughly every 30 minutes. A field mouse reaches breeding age at about 6 weeks.',
          prompt: 'Calculate roughly how many bacterial generations pass in a single week, and how many mouse generations pass in that same week. Use your two figures to explain why a bacterial population can evolve antibiotic resistance within a patient in days, while a comparable adaptation in the mouse population would take far longer.',
          answer: 'Bacteria: a week is 7 × 24 = 168 hours, and at one division every half-hour that is about 336 generations. Mouse: a generation is 6 weeks, so in one week only about 1/6 of a generation passes — fewer than one. In that single week the bacteria pass through roughly 2 000 times as many generations as the mouse. Evolutionary change is counted in generations, not in days, because each generation is one opportunity for new mutations to arise and one round in which natural selection can shift allele frequencies. The bacterial population also runs to enormous numbers, so even a mutation of very low probability per division is near-certain to appear somewhere in the population. So when an antibiotic is applied, a resistant variant is likely to already be present; it survives, reproduces, and within a few hundred generations — a few days of real time — resistant cells dominate the population. The mouse population is not evolving by any different mechanism, and is not slower per generation; it simply gets through so few generations per unit of real time that the equivalent shift in allele frequency is spread over decades.',
          explanation: 'The calculation carries the argument, so do it before reasoning. The mark that separates a strong answer is recognising that generation time, not any difference in mechanism, is what makes bacterial evolution observable on a human timescale — bacteria are not evolving in a special way, they are simply running the same process thousands of times faster. Population size matters too: huge numbers make even a rare mutation likely to be present when the antibiotic arrives, so selection usually acts on variation that is ALREADY there rather than waiting for it.',
