          grade: 12,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'A human embryo at about five weeks has a tail containing 10 to 12 vertebrae, clearly visible and proportionally about one sixth of the embryo. By around eight weeks most of these vertebrae have been removed by programmed cell death, and the remaining three to five fuse into the coccyx. Rarely, a baby is born with the tail still present.',
          prompt: 'A learner writes that this shows "a human embryo passes through the adult stages of its ancestors — first a fish, then a reptile, then a human". Evaluate that statement, and explain why the coccyx is stronger evidence for evolution than a structure that is merely small and unused.',
          answer: 'The statement is the old idea that development repeats ancestral adult forms, and it is wrong, although what it is trying to describe is real. An embryo never passes through the ADULT stage of any ancestor. What it shares with the embryos of related animals is early developmental stages and the machinery that builds them, because those stages are inherited along with everything else and changing them disturbs everything built afterwards. So the human embryo resembles other vertebrate EMBRYOS, not adult fish or adult reptiles, and the resemblance is evidence of shared ancestry rather than of a replayed history. What the tail sequence genuinely indicates: a human embryo at five weeks builds a tail of 10 to 12 vertebrae, about a sixth of its length. The developmental programme for building a vertebrate tail is present and is switched on. It is then partly dismantled — most vertebrae removed by programmed cell death, the remaining three to five fused into the coccyx — and very rarely the removal fails and a baby is born with the tail. The inherited instructions for a tail are therefore still in the human genome and still run. Why this is stronger evidence than a merely small structure: a structure that is simply small and unused invites the reply that it was always small and was built that way for some purpose we have not identified. That reply is not available here, because the tail is BUILT AT FULL PROPORTION FIRST AND THEN REMOVED. Constructing something elaborate only to destroy it makes no sense as a design and makes complete sense as a history: the building programme was inherited from tailed ancestors and a later modification suppresses it. The occasional failure of the suppression, producing a baby with a tail, is the confirmation.',
