          answer:
            'If individuals do not mate entirely at random, but instead preferentially choose mates that share (or differ in) a particular heritable characteristic, then certain combinations of alleles will be passed on to the next generation more or less frequently than would be expected under purely random mating. Over successive generations, this preferential pairing can shift the frequency of the alleles involved, or change the proportion of homozygous versus heterozygous individuals in the population, compared to what random mating would otherwise produce.',
          explanation: 'This shows that non-random mating is a further mechanism (in addition to natural selection, mutation, genetic drift and gene flow) by which allele and genotype frequencies within a population can change from one generation to the next, without requiring any difference in survival or overall reproductive success between genotypes.',
