          marks: 6,
          context: 'A species of small fish lived throughout a river system. A new river channel cut through, splitting the system into a fast, rocky northern stretch and a slow, muddy southern stretch, with no route between them. Ten thousand years later the northern fish are slender with large fins; the southern fish are deep-bodied and drab. Researchers reunited the two forms in a tank: they bred readily and the offspring were healthy and fertile.',
          prompt: 'Explain what has happened to this fish population, and use the breeding result to decide whether allopatric speciation is complete. Give a reason for your decision.',
          answer: 'The new channel acted as a geographic barrier, splitting one interbreeding population into two between which gene flow stopped. Each then evolved independently under its own conditions: fast rocky water favours a slender body and large fins for control in the current, while slow muddy water favours a deep body and drab colouring, so different selection pressures, together with separate mutation and drift, drove the two forms apart in appearance. Speciation, however, is NOT complete. Under the biological species concept two populations are separate species only when they can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring, and these fish bred readily and produced healthy, fertile young. So despite ten thousand years of separation and a marked difference in appearance, the two remain one species — they have diverged, but reproductive isolation has not yet arisen. This also means the divergence is reversible: were the two stretches to reconnect, the populations would interbreed and gene flow would resume, blending the two forms back together.',
          explanation: 'This is the mirror image of the case where the reunited animals fail to breed, and comparing the two is the point. Geographic isolation and visible divergence are the START of allopatric speciation, not proof of it; only reproductive isolation completes the process. The strongest answers draw the consequence — a divergence not yet locked in by reproductive isolation can be undone by reconnection, which is why speciation is a threshold and not simply a matter of accumulated difference.',
