          prompt: 'A learner argues that the ostrich wing cannot be vestigial, "because vestigial means useless, and the ostrich obviously uses it". Evaluate the learner’s argument, and state what the bone arrangement indicates about the ancestry of the ostrich.',
          answer: 'The argument rests on a wrong definition, so it fails. Vestigial does not mean useless. A vestigial structure is one that is greatly REDUCED compared with the fully functional version in related species, and no longer performs its ANCESTRAL function. The ostrich wing has lost the function it is built for — the flight muscles are greatly reduced and the wing cannot generate lift — and that is what makes it vestigial. Being put to other uses does not change this. Braking, steering while running and courtship display are new functions taken on by a structure that was already there; they are not flight, and a structure is not classified by whether it does something but by whether it still does the thing its form is evidence of. Vestigial structures very often acquire secondary uses, and the coccyx, which anchors muscles of the pelvic floor, is another example. What the bones indicate: the wing contains the same humerus, radius, ulna and reduced digits, in the same arrangement, as a flying bird’s wing. That shared pattern is a homologous structure, and the most economical explanation of it is common ancestry — the ostrich descends from an ancestor that flew, and has retained the skeletal plan while losing the function. If the wing had been built from scratch for braking and display there would be no reason for it to be a flight limb in miniature. Note that the learner’s argument, if accepted, would make "vestigial" almost an empty category, since natural selection tends to find some use for whatever is available.',
