          answer: 'The claim is wrong, and the two populations are separate species. What has happened since the mountains rose: a single continuous population was divided by a barrier the rodents cannot cross, so gene flow between the two sides stopped. Each population then accumulated its own mutations and was subject to its own selection pressures — the wetter west favouring larger bodies and dark fur, the drier east favouring smaller, paler animals — and over two million years they diverged. That is allopatric speciation. Why the breeding result is decisive rather than inconclusive: the learner has taken "they mated" as the whole test, and it is not. The test for separate species is whether the two can produce FERTILE OFFSPRING, and here they produced no offspring at all. Courting and mating normally shows only that the behavioural barriers to mating have not diverged — the animals still recognise each other as potential mates. The isolation is at a later stage: fertilisation failing, or the zygote not developing, most likely because two million years of separate change have left the chromosomes unable to pair properly. Producing no viable young is as complete a reproductive isolation as refusing to mate, and it is the outcome, not the willingness, that the definition turns on. Note how strong this evidence is: the researchers did not have to argue from geography at all. They removed the barrier by housing the animals together, so the failure to breed cannot be explained by the mountains, which is precisely the test that separates a genuinely new species from two populations that are merely apart.',
