          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'Yeast is grown in sugar solution in two sealed flasks. Flask 1 is bubbled with air throughout; Flask 2 has all air excluded. After 24 hours, Flask 1 contains far more yeast cells and no alcohol. Flask 2 contains fewer cells and a measurable concentration of alcohol. Both flasks started with the same amount of sugar, and both have used it all.',
          prompt: 'Explain why the same amount of sugar produced so many more cells in Flask 1, and explain what the alcohol in Flask 2 tells you about how completely the sugar was broken down.',
          answer: 'Both flasks respire the same sugar, but they release very different amounts of energy from it. With oxygen present, Flask 1 respires aerobically: glucose is broken down completely to carbon dioxide and water, releasing a large quantity of ATP per glucose molecule. Growth and cell division cost energy, so more ATP means more new cells, which is why the same sugar supported far more yeast. Without oxygen, Flask 2 must ferment: glucose is broken down only to ethanol and carbon dioxide, releasing very little ATP per glucose molecule, so the same sugar funded far fewer divisions. The alcohol is the evidence of that incompleteness. Ethanol is still an energy-containing organic molecule — it burns — so the fact that it is left over shows the yeast could not extract all the energy from the glucose and abandoned the breakdown partway. Aerobic respiration leaves carbon dioxide and water, neither of which holds usable energy, which is the sign that the molecule was taken all the way. So the waste product itself tells you how far the process went.',
          explanation: 'Trace the energy rather than the gas: the difference in cell numbers is a measure of ATP yield, since growth is what the yeast spends ATP on. The strongest move in the second half is recognising that ETHANOL STILL CONTAINS ENERGY — a fuel as a waste product is direct evidence of incomplete breakdown, and it is also, incidentally, why brewing works at all.',
