          context: 'An athlete runs a 400 m race in under a minute and finishes barely able to stand, with burning legs. Another athlete runs a marathon for over three hours and finishes tired but able to keep moving. Both were respiring throughout.',
          prompt: 'Explain why a race lasting under a minute leaves the muscles in a worse state than one lasting three hours, referring to the type of respiration each athlete relied on.',
          answer: 'The difference is whether the oxygen supply could keep up with the demand. The marathon runner works below the rate at which their heart and lungs can deliver oxygen, so the muscles respire aerobically throughout: glucose is broken down completely, the waste products are carbon dioxide and water, both of which are removed easily, and nothing accumulates. The pace is sustainable precisely because it is matched to oxygen delivery, which is why three hours is possible. The 400 m runner works far above that rate. Oxygen cannot be delivered fast enough, so the muscles supplement aerobic respiration with anaerobic respiration, which yields far less ATP per glucose and produces lactic acid. The lactic acid accumulates in the muscle tissue, lowering its pH, which interferes with the enzymes and the contraction mechanism and causes the burning sensation and the loss of power. So the shorter race is more damaging because its intensity forces a pathway that leaves a harmful product behind, while the longer one, though far greater in total work, never exceeds the oxygen supply and leaves nothing to clear up.',
          explanation: 'The comparison turns on RATE rather than total effort, which is the point most easily missed: the marathon involves vastly more work overall and still leaves the muscles in better condition, because the work was done within the oxygen supply. It is also worth noticing that anaerobic respiration is not a failure but a stopgap — it buys power the oxygen supply cannot fund, at the cost of a product that then has to be dealt with.',
