          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Describe the structure of the trachea, and explain the function of the C-shaped rings of cartilage found along its length.',
          answer: 'The trachea is a tube lined with ciliated epithelial cells and mucus-secreting goblet cells, reinforced along its length by a series of C-shaped rings of cartilage. These cartilage rings keep the trachea permanently open (preventing it from collapsing), while the gap in each "C" (facing the oesophagus) allows the oesophagus to expand slightly during swallowing.',
          explanation: 'Without this cartilage support, the trachea (a soft-walled tube) would be at risk of collapsing under the pressure changes involved in breathing, which would obstruct the essential airflow to and from the lungs.',
