          answer:
            '(1) Alveoli have very thin walls (a single layer of flattened epithelial cells), minimising the distance over which gases must diffuse. (2) Alveoli are surrounded by a dense network of blood capillaries, also with thin walls, maintaining a strong diffusion gradient by continuously carrying oxygen away and carbon dioxide toward the alveoli. (3) The huge number of alveoli in the lungs provides an enormous total surface area for gaseous exchange to occur across.',
          explanation: 'These same three general features — a short diffusion distance, a well-maintained concentration gradient, and a large surface area — are the hallmark adaptations of essentially every efficient exchange surface found in living organisms.',
