          prompt: 'Assess the relative\'s argument. Justify your answer by referring to what determines the rate of gaseous exchange.',
          answer: 'The measurement is correct and the inference from it is wrong, because volume is not what gaseous exchange depends on. In emphysema the walls between adjacent alveoli break down, so many small air sacs merge into fewer, larger ones. That does increase the volume of air the lungs hold, and it also makes the lungs less elastic so air is trapped, which is why the test reads high. But exchange happens across the alveolar surface, and its rate depends on the total surface area available, on the thinness of the barrier and on the concentration gradient. A given volume divided into very many small sacs presents an enormous surface; the same volume as a few large ones presents far less. So merging alveoli destroys surface area while increasing volume -- the two move in opposite directions, which is exactly what the relative has not allowed for. Less surface means less oxygen diffuses into the blood per breath, the patient breathes harder to compensate, and the trapped air makes each breath mechanically harder as well. The breathlessness is therefore a lung problem, and the high reading is one of its signs rather than evidence against it.',
