          answer: '(1) At some stage of its life (even if only as an embryo), a chordate possesses a notochord — a flexible, rod-like structure running along the length of its body, providing structural support. (2) A chordate also possesses a dorsal (hollow) nerve cord, running along its back, above the notochord.',
          explanation: 'In vertebrates (a subgroup of chordates), the notochord is typically replaced by a bony or cartilaginous vertebral column (backbone) during development, but the dorsal nerve cord persists as the spinal cord — both structures are inherited, evolutionarily, from this shared chordate body plan.',
