          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Compare the body segmentation of an animal in the phylum Annelida (such as an earthworm) with that of an animal in the phylum Arthropoda (such as an insect).',
          answer: 'Annelids (such as earthworms) show a body divided into many similar, repeating segments, with few or no specialised external appendages differing between segments. Arthropods (such as insects) also show a segmented body, but their segments are typically fused into distinct body regions (such as a head, thorax and abdomen in insects), each bearing jointed appendages that are often specialised for different functions (such as legs for walking, or antennae for sensing).',
          explanation: 'This difference — largely uniform, repeating segments in annelids, versus fused, specialised body regions with jointed, functionally differentiated appendages in arthropods — reflects a generally more complex level of body organisation in arthropods compared to annelids.',
