          explanation: 'The membrane in a dialyser is not clever — it lets small molecules through in both directions, and what is REMOVED is decided by what the dialysis fluid already contains. That is why the fluid holds normal glucose and salts: to stop the patient losing them. The counter-current point is the same arrangement used by fish gills and by the loop of Henle, and the reason is identical in all three: it prevents the gradient from collapsing partway along.',
