          marks: 5,
          context: 'A patient with severe kidney damage and a high blood urea level asks whether they could avoid dialysis by simply eating no protein at all, since urea comes from protein.',
          prompt: 'Assess the patient\'s proposal. Justify your answer by explaining where urea comes from and why a high level is harmful.',
          answer: 'The chemistry behind the suggestion is right and following it would be dangerous, so it cannot be adopted as stated. Urea is made in the liver from the amino acids the body cannot store: excess amino acids are deaminated, the amino group is converted to ammonia, which is highly toxic, and the liver converts that ammonia to the far less toxic urea for the kidneys to excrete. So dietary protein genuinely is the source, and reducing it does lower urea production -- which is why a moderate, medically supervised protein restriction is a real part of managing kidney disease. Eating no protein at all is a different matter. Protein is not optional: it is needed to build and repair tissues, to make enzymes, antibodies and hormones, and there is no store of it to draw on. A person eating none begins breaking down their own muscle to supply the amino acids their body requires, and those amino acids are deaminated in turn -- so urea is still produced, now at the cost of the patient\'s own tissue. They would waste away while the blood urea failed to fall as hoped. And urea is only part of the problem dialysis solves. Failing kidneys also lose control of water and salt balance, so fluid accumulates and potassium rises, and a high potassium level can stop the heart -- which diet alone does not address. Untreated, accumulating urea and other nitrogenous wastes cause uraemia, affecting brain function, and kidney failure is fatal without dialysis or a transplant. So the suggestion identifies the right pathway and the wrong lever.',
