          cognitiveLevel: 1,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Describe TWO key characteristics used to identify an animal as belonging to the phylum Echinodermata (for example, starfish and sea urchins).',
          answer: '(1) Adult echinoderms typically show five-part (pentaradial) radial symmetry, with body parts arranged in five (or a multiple of five) sections around a central point, despite their larvae usually being bilaterally symmetrical. (2) Echinoderms possess a unique water vascular system — an internal network of fluid-filled canals used to operate numerous small, fluid-filled tube feet, which are used for movement, feeding and gas exchange.',
          explanation: 'The water vascular system and tube feet are unique to echinoderms among all animal phyla, and represent a highly distinctive method of movement and feeding not found in any other group of animals.',
