          answer: 'The trachea divides into two bronchi, one leading into each lung, which then branch repeatedly into a network of progressively narrower tubes called bronchioles. This branching network carries air deep into the lungs, ultimately delivering it to the alveoli at the end of the smallest bronchioles, where gaseous exchange takes place.',
          explanation: 'This repeatedly branching structure, often compared to an upside-down tree, allows air to be distributed evenly and efficiently to the enormous number of alveoli spread throughout both lungs.',
