          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'A clinic in a busy township is under pressure. Patients with coughs and colds expect antibiotics and become angry when refused, and a nurse argues that prescribing them keeps patients returning to the clinic for the serious conditions that matter, so the harm is outweighed.',
          prompt: 'Evaluate the nurse\'s argument. Justify your answer by explaining how overuse leads to resistant bacteria.',
          answer: 'The nurse\'s concern is legitimate and the remedy chosen makes the clinic\'s own work harder in the long run. Bacterial populations vary in susceptibility; whenever an antibiotic is used, the susceptible are killed and any less susceptible individuals survive and reproduce, so resistance becomes commoner in the population that remains. That is selection, and it happens every time the drug is used -- which is why unnecessary use is not neutral but actively harmful. Prescribing for coughs and colds is the clearest case of unnecessary use, because those are overwhelmingly viral and antibiotics have no effect on viruses at all: the patient gets no benefit whatsoever, while the bacteria they carry, including harmless ones that can pass resistance genes on to pathogens, are selected for resistance. The resistant strains then spread between people, so the cost lands on the clinic\'s own patients, who will later present with infections that no longer respond. Against that, the nurse is right that trust matters and that a patient who stops attending may go untreated for something serious, which is a real harm and not a rhetorical one. But the argument concedes the wrong thing: what maintains trust is an explanation and a plan -- why antibiotics will not help a virus, what will help the symptoms, and when to come back if it worsens -- rather than a prescription that buys goodwill by spending the effectiveness of the drug. Where the clinic genuinely lacks the time for that conversation, that is the problem to raise, and it is not solved by prescribing.',
