          marks: 4,
          context: 'A smallholder\'s maize wilts and grows poorly in a dry season. A neighbour advises spraying the leaves with water twice a day, reasoning that since water is a raw material for photosynthesis, wetting the leaves supplies it directly where it is needed.',
          prompt: 'Evaluate the neighbour\'s advice. Justify your answer by explaining how a shortage of soil water reduces photosynthesis.',
          answer: 'The advice misidentifies why the drought hurts the plant, so it treats the wrong thing. Water is indeed a raw material, but the plant needs remarkably little of it for that purpose; the great majority of the water taken up is lost by transpiration through the stomata. That is the connection the neighbour has missed. When soil water is short, the plant closes its stomata to reduce that loss, and the stomata are also the pores through which carbon dioxide enters the leaf. With them closed, carbon dioxide cannot get in, it becomes the limiting factor, and the rate of photosynthesis falls -- so the shortage acts on the supply of carbon dioxide far more than on the supply of water as a reactant. A water shortage also causes the cells to lose turgor, so leaves wilt and present less surface to the light. Spraying the leaves addresses neither: leaves absorb very little water through their surface, the roots are where uptake happens, and a wet leaf surface does nothing to reopen stomata that are closed because the soil is dry. It may even do harm, by encouraging fungal growth on damp leaves and by wasting scarce water to evaporation off a surface rather than into the soil. What the crop needs is water at the roots -- watering the soil, preferably early or late to reduce evaporation, and mulching to keep it there.',
