          answer: 'Urea is produced in the liver, through the breakdown (deamination) of excess amino acids that the body does not need or cannot store, which removes the nitrogen-containing amino group and converts it into the less toxic compound urea. This urea is released into the blood, which carries it to the kidneys, where it is filtered out of the blood at the glomerulus and, since it is not reabsorbed, remains in the filtrate to be excreted in the urine.',
          explanation: 'This shows that the kidneys do not actually produce urea themselves — they are purely responsible for removing it from the blood once the liver has already produced it, illustrating how excretion often depends on cooperation between more than one organ.',
