          label: '5.5',
          topicId: 'life-sci-excretion',
          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Explain why urine is normally more concentrated (contains a higher solute concentration) than blood plasma.',
          answer: 'As filtrate passes along the nephron, a large proportion of the water originally filtered out at the glomerulus is selectively reabsorbed back into the blood, particularly in the proximal convoluted tubule and, later, the collecting duct, while waste substances such as urea are largely left behind. Because a similar amount of waste solute is now contained within a much smaller remaining volume of liquid, the resulting urine ends up considerably more concentrated than the original blood plasma it was filtered from.',
          explanation: 'This ability to concentrate urine well above the concentration of blood plasma is essential for conserving water — if urine were never more concentrated than plasma, the body would be forced to lose far larger volumes of water each day simply to excrete the same amount of waste solute.',
