          prompt: 'Explain, in general terms, how the kidneys help to regulate the pH of the blood.',
          answer:
            'The normal chemical reactions of the body’s metabolism continuously produce acidic waste products, including hydrogen ions, which would lower blood pH if simply allowed to accumulate. As blood passes through the kidneys, excess hydrogen ions are actively secreted from the blood into the filtrate within the nephron tubules, to be excreted in the urine, while the kidneys can also reabsorb bicarbonate ions back into the blood to help buffer (neutralise) any remaining acidity. Together, these processes allow the kidneys to continuously remove excess acid from the blood, helping to keep blood pH within the narrow range needed for normal enzyme function throughout the body.',
          explanation: 'This shows that the kidneys’ role extends well beyond water and salt balance alone — by also regulating blood pH, they act as a second major system (alongside breathing rate, which removes acidic carbon dioxide) contributing to the overall acid-base balance of the body.',
