          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'A frog begins life as a tadpole and ends it as an adult. The tadpole has gills, a long coiled gut, a finned tail and no limbs. The adult has lungs and a moist permeable skin, a short gut, four limbs and no tail. The tadpole eats algae; the adult eats insects.',
          prompt: 'Explain how this change of body suits the frog to two different ways of life, and explain why an adult frog must stay in damp places even though it has lungs.',
          answer: 'The tadpole is built for water and for plant food. Gills extract dissolved oxygen, which is the only option for an animal living wholly submerged; the finned tail propels it by side-to-side beating; and the long coiled gut provides the length and the time needed to digest algae, whose cellulose walls are difficult to break down. The adult is built for land and for animal food. Lungs take oxygen from air; four limbs support the body against gravity and allow it to jump, which a tail would only hinder; and a short gut is sufficient because insect tissue is far easier and quicker to digest than algae. Changing body allows one animal to exploit two habitats and two food supplies in one lifetime, and it also means the young do not compete with the adults for food. The adult must stay damp because its lungs are not its only gas-exchange surface. A frog also absorbs a large proportion of its oxygen through its skin, which must be thin, permeable and moist for gases to dissolve and diffuse across it. That same permeability means water is lost from the skin readily in dry air, so a frog in the open dries out and its skin stops working for gas exchange. Its lungs are not efficient enough to supply it alone, which is why amphibians remain tied to damp habitats even as adults.',
