          marks: 5,
          context: 'A patient has chronic pancreatitis, in which the pancreas is progressively destroyed. Their doctor prescribes both enzyme capsules to be swallowed with every meal AND daily insulin injections.',
          prompt: 'Explain why the damage to a single organ produces these two quite different treatments, referring to the two kinds of gland tissue the pancreas contains.',
          answer: 'The pancreas is two glands in one organ, and destroying the organ damages both. Most of it is exocrine tissue, which makes digestive enzymes — amylase, lipase and protease — and releases them through a duct into the small intestine, where they act on food outside the bloodstream. A much smaller part is endocrine tissue, the islets, which makes the hormones insulin and glucagon and releases them directly into the blood to act on cells elsewhere in the body. The two treatments replace the two outputs. The enzyme capsules replace the exocrine secretion, and they are swallowed with meals because the enzymes have to reach the food in the gut and act there — the timing matters, since enzymes taken between meals would have nothing to work on. The insulin is injected rather than swallowed because it is a hormone that must reach the blood and act on liver and muscle cells; and because it is a protein, swallowing it would simply expose it to the patient’s own protease and have it digested before it could act. The two routes of delivery therefore mirror the two routes of secretion the healthy pancreas uses.',
          explanation: 'Exocrine and endocrine differ in where the product is released — into a duct, or into the blood — and the two prescriptions read straight off that distinction once it is held clearly. The detail that repays thought is WHY insulin cannot be a tablet: it is a protein, and the digestive system exists to break proteins down, which is the same fact that made insulin injections necessary long before anyone could manufacture the hormone.',
