          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 4,
          context: 'After having their gall bladder removed, a patient is told they can eat normally. They find that large fatty meals now cause discomfort and loose stools, but several small meals through the day do not. The patient concludes the surgeon must have made an error.',
          prompt: 'Assess the patient\'s conclusion. Justify your answer by explaining what the gall bladder does and what changes without it.',
          answer: 'The conclusion is almost certainly wrong, and the pattern the patient has noticed is in fact the evidence against it. The liver, not the gall bladder, makes bile; the gall bladder stores it and concentrates it, then releases a large quantity at once when fatty food arrives in the duodenum. Bile emulsifies fat, breaking large globules into many tiny droplets, which greatly increases the surface area available to lipase and so allows fat to be digested at a reasonable rate. Removing the gall bladder does not stop bile production -- it continues to trickle steadily from the liver into the duodenum -- so fat digestion still happens, which is why the patient can eat normally most of the time. What is lost is the ability to deliver a large surge of bile on demand. A large fatty meal therefore arrives faster than the steady trickle can emulsify it, so some fat passes on incompletely digested, which causes exactly the discomfort and loose stools described. The fact that small frequent meals are comfortable is the clue: it shows the system works and is limited in rate, not that it is broken, which is what a surgical error would look like. The sensible response is to match meal size to the delivery the patient now has, and to raise it with the doctor if symptoms persist -- but on this evidence the surgery did what it was meant to.',
