          context: 'A swimming coach tells a squad that the best way to improve underwater swimming is to hyperventilate -- take several very deep, rapid breaths -- immediately before submerging, because this loads the blood with extra oxygen and allows a longer breath-hold. Several national federations ban the practice at their pools.',
          prompt: 'Evaluate the coach\'s claim, and assess whether the ban is justified. Refer to what limits a breath-hold and to how muscle cells produce ATP once oxygen runs short.',
          answer: 'The claim is wrong about the mechanism in a way that makes the practice dangerous, so the ban is justified. Hyperventilation adds very little oxygen: haemoglobin leaving the lungs is already almost fully saturated during normal breathing, so there is little spare carrying capacity to fill. What it does do is blow off carbon dioxide, lowering its concentration in the blood -- and that matters because it is rising carbon dioxide, not falling oxygen, that the body monitors to trigger the overwhelming urge to breathe. So the longer breath-hold is real but it is not extra oxygen; it is a delayed warning. During the swim the muscles respire aerobically while oxygen lasts, and as it runs short they shift to anaerobic respiration, which yields only a small amount of ATP per glucose and produces lactic acid, so the muscles fatigue quickly. Meanwhile oxygen continues to fall, and because the carbon dioxide signal was artificially suppressed, the swimmer may reach the point where the brain is starved of oxygen before they feel any urge to surface -- losing consciousness underwater, without warning, in shallow water. That is why federations ban it, and the ban is well founded: the risk is drowning in a swimmer who feels fine seconds beforehand, and the supposed benefit rests on a mechanism that does not operate. The legitimate way to improve is training that raises aerobic capacity and tolerance of lactate.',
