          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'A person with a severe lung infection (pneumonia) develops a build-up of fluid within their alveoli. Explain why this impairs gaseous exchange.',
          answer: 'Fluid filling the alveoli increases the distance that oxygen and carbon dioxide must diffuse across between the air (or, once flooded, the fluid) and the blood in the surrounding capillaries, since gases diffuse far more slowly through liquid than through air. This fluid can also physically prevent fresh air from reaching the affected alveoli at all in severe cases, both of which reduce the rate at which oxygen can be taken up into, and carbon dioxide removed from, the blood.',
