          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain why glucose, unlike urea, is normally almost completely reabsorbed during selective reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule.',
          answer:
            'Glucose is a valuable nutrient the body needs to retain, so the cells lining the proximal convoluted tubule actively transport essentially all of the glucose present in the filtrate back into the surrounding blood capillaries, using specific glucose transport proteins. Urea, by contrast, is a waste product the body needs to remove, so it is not actively reabsorbed in the same way, and largely remains within the filtrate to be excreted in the urine, along with only a small amount that diffuses back passively.',
          explanation: 'This difference illustrates that selective reabsorption is genuinely selective at the molecular level — the tubule cells possess specific transport mechanisms for substances the body wants to keep, while having no equivalent mechanism to actively recover a waste substance such as urea.',
