          cognitiveLevel: 1,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Describe the role of the hypothalamus in controlling the release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH).',
          answer: 'The hypothalamus contains specialised cells that continuously monitor the water content (concentration) of the blood. When blood water content is low (the blood is too concentrated), the hypothalamus signals the pituitary gland to release more ADH into the blood; when blood water content is high, the hypothalamus signals the pituitary gland to release less ADH.',
