          context: 'A rodent collected from an unknown habitat has very small eyes under a covering of skin, broad forelimbs with long heavy claws, a cylindrical body with short dense fur that lies flat in any direction, almost no external ear flaps, and a high tolerance of carbon dioxide in its blood.',
          prompt: 'Deduce the habitat this animal is adapted to, and explain how each of THREE of these features suits it to that habitat.',
          answer: 'The animal is adapted to a burrowing, underground life. Several features point the same way. The broad forelimbs with long heavy claws are digging tools, giving a wide surface to push soil and the strength to shift it. The short dense fur lying flat in any direction lets the animal move forwards and backwards along a tight tunnel without the coat catching or being rubbed the wrong way, which matters because a burrow gives no room to turn round. The reduced ears sit flush with the body for the same reason: projecting ear flaps would snag on tunnel walls, and they would be of little use where sound matters less than touch and smell. The tolerance of carbon dioxide is the most telling feature of all, because it is not a digging adaptation but a response to the ATMOSPHERE of a burrow: air in a sealed tunnel is not exchanged with the outside, so carbon dioxide accumulates to concentrations that would distress a surface-dwelling mammal. Only an animal spending most of its life underground would be under selection to tolerate it. The small, skin-covered eyes fit as well, since eyes are of little use in the dark and are vulnerable to soil, so selection reduces rather than maintains them.',
          explanation: 'Work from the evidence to the habitat, not the other way round, and check that the features agree with one another — a single trait could be coincidence, five pointing one way is a deduction. The carbon dioxide tolerance is the feature worth dwelling on: it says nothing about digging and everything about living in air that does not circulate, which is why it identifies the habitat more precisely than the claws do.',
