          prompt: 'Describe the role of goblet cells and ciliated cells in the lining of the trachea.',
          answer: 'Goblet cells secrete a sticky layer of mucus that coats the inner lining of the trachea, trapping inhaled dust, bacteria and other airborne particles before they can travel further into the lungs. Ciliated cells possess tiny hair-like cilia on their surface, which beat in a coordinated, wave-like motion to continuously sweep this mucus, along with the particles it has trapped, upward toward the throat, where it can be swallowed or coughed out.',
