          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain, in terms of pressure changes, why air is drawn into the lungs during inhalation rather than being actively "pulled" in by the breathing muscles themselves.',
          answer:
            'The breathing muscles do not act directly on the air itself; instead, their contraction increases the volume of the thoracic cavity. Because the same amount of air now occupies a larger volume, the air pressure inside the lungs decreases, becoming lower than the surrounding atmospheric air pressure outside the body. Air then flows naturally from this region of higher pressure outside to the region of lower pressure inside the lungs, down the pressure gradient created — it is not physically pulled in by the muscles themselves.',
          explanation: 'This distinction matters because it shows that breathing works entirely through indirect, passive gas movement following a pressure gradient — the muscles’ only direct effect is to change thoracic volume, with the resulting airflow being a passive physical consequence of that volume change.',
