          answer:
            '(1) The trachea divides into two main bronchi, one entering each lung, ensuring both lungs receive an air supply. (2) Each bronchus branches repeatedly into progressively smaller and more numerous bronchioles, spreading air throughout the entire volume of each lung. (3) The smallest bronchioles end in clusters of alveoli, placing the final stretch of the airway in close proximity to a very large total number of individual alveoli.',
          explanation: 'This highly branched, tree-like structure allows a single trachea to efficiently distribute air to the hundreds of millions of individual alveoli spread throughout both lungs, without requiring an impractically large number of separate, independent airways.',
