          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Compare the structure of the trachea with the structure of a bronchiole, explaining how each is suited to its position and function within the respiratory system.',
          answer:
            'The trachea, being a large, central airway that must remain open under significant and continuously changing air pressure, is supported by rigid C-shaped rings of cartilage, preventing it from collapsing. Bronchioles, being much smaller, numerous airways found deep within the lung tissue where they are already well supported by the surrounding lung tissue itself, generally lack cartilage; instead, their walls contain smooth muscle, which can contract or relax to actively narrow or widen the bronchiole’s diameter, allowing fine control over airflow to different parts of the lung.',
