          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Explain why a collapsed lung (pneumothorax), in which air leaks into the space between the pleural membranes, prevents that lung from functioning normally.',
          answer: 'The seal formed by the pleural fluid between the two pleural membranes normally allows the lung to expand and contract passively in step with the movements of the rib cage and diaphragm. If air leaks into this space, it breaks this seal, and the lung, being naturally elastic, recoils and collapses inward on itself, no longer able to follow the volume changes of the thoracic cavity. As a result, that lung can no longer draw in or expel air normally, severely reducing the amount of gaseous exchange it can carry out.',
          explanation: 'This illustrates why the integrity of the pleural seal, though easy to overlook compared to more obviously vital structures such as the alveoli, is nonetheless essential for normal lung function — without it, the mechanical link between chest wall movement and lung expansion is lost entirely.',
