          answer: 'The immunologist has the stronger case, and the reason lies in what immunity is actually to. Recovering from an infection leaves memory cells that recognise the specific surface antigens of that particular pathogen, so a second encounter with the same one is met by a fast, large antibody response and usually causes no illness. Immunity is therefore not to "colds" but to the individual strain that caused each one. The common cold is caused by well over a hundred distinct rhinovirus strains, each with different surface antigens, and the antibodies raised against one do not recognise another -- so a person can recover fully and be reinfected the following month by a strain their memory cells have never seen. A vaccine faces exactly the same obstacle: it would have to cover a very large number of strains at once, and rhinoviruses also mutate, so the target keeps moving. Against a single stable virus with few strains, one vaccine can confer lasting protection, which is far more health gained per rand. The argument is not unanswerable -- the cold imposes an enormous total burden in lost work and school days, and research aimed at parts of the virus shared across strains could in principle yield broad protection -- so a defensible reply exists. But on the evidence as presented, the immunologist is right that strain diversity makes this a poor target compared with the alternative.',
