          marks: 4,
          context: 'A coach has two athletes of equal current fitness preparing for an 800 m race, which is run near the limit of what aerobic respiration can supply. Athlete A is given a programme of long, steady-paced runs. Athlete B is given a programme of repeated short, very hard efforts with short recoveries. The coach claims that only programme A can improve an athlete, because only aerobic training builds the systems that deliver oxygen.',
          prompt: 'Evaluate the coach\'s claim, referring to what each kind of training changes in the body.',
          answer: 'The coach has identified something real and then over-claimed from it. Programme A does exactly what the coach says: sustained aerobic work increases the density of capillaries supplying the muscles and the number of mitochondria within the muscle cells, so oxygen is both delivered and used faster. That raises the intensity at which an athlete can still meet demand aerobically, which delays the point at which anaerobic respiration must take over, and with it the accumulation of lactate and the fatigue that follows. But "only aerobic training can improve an athlete" does not follow, and the 800 m is the event that shows why: it is run above the intensity aerobic respiration can supply, so some anaerobic contribution is unavoidable no matter how well trained the athlete is. Programme B trains what happens then -- the muscles\' tolerance of the acidic conditions lactate produces, and the rate at which the body clears and re-uses it during recovery -- which determines how long the athlete can hold that pace once the aerobic ceiling is passed. Neither programme is sufficient alone. The defensible position is that A raises the ceiling and B extends how far above it the athlete can go, and an 800 m runner needs both; the coach\'s error is treating the aerobic adaptation as the only kind of improvement there is.',
