          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain how the structure of a bacterium’s cell wall relates to whether it is classified as gram-positive or gram-negative, and describe the general significance of this distinction.',
          answer:
            'Gram-positive bacteria have a thick layer of peptidoglycan in their cell wall, which retains a violet-coloured stain (crystal violet) during the Gram staining procedure, causing them to appear purple under a microscope. Gram-negative bacteria have a much thinner peptidoglycan layer, surrounded by an additional outer membrane, which does not retain the violet stain as effectively, so a counterstain (safranin) is used instead, causing them to appear pink/red. This distinction is significant because it reflects real structural differences that affect how bacteria respond to certain antibiotics (some antibiotics target peptidoglycan synthesis, and are therefore more effective against gram-positive bacteria, which rely more heavily on this thick layer for structural support) and because gram staining provides doctors and microbiologists with a rapid, simple first step in identifying an unknown bacterial infection.',
          explanation: 'The Gram stain remains one of the most widely used, rapid diagnostic tools in microbiology precisely because it reveals a genuine underlying structural difference in cell wall composition, rather than being an arbitrary or superficial classification.',
