          answer: 'It is better adapted to conditions that hold, and worse adapted to conditions that change, so the report\'s phrase is too simple to be either right or wrong on its own. What specialisation gains is efficiency. Because flower and pollinator have evolved together, the structure attracts, rewards and physically fits one species: the bird\'s bill matches the curve, the nectar sits where only that bill reaches, and the anthers touch the bird in the same place every visit. Pollen is therefore carried to another flower of the same species rather than wasted on unrelated ones, so less pollen achieves more pollination, and the plant need not produce as much of it. The generalist plant wastes far more pollen on visitors that carry it nowhere useful. What specialisation costs is dependence. The plant\'s entire reproduction rests on one species continuing to be present, in the right numbers, at the right time of year. If the sunbird declines through habitat loss, disease or a shift in the season, the specialist has no alternative and its seed set collapses, while the generalist simply loses some of its many visitors and keeps reproducing through the rest. So specialisation is a trade of resilience for efficiency, and which is "better" depends on how stable the environment is -- which is why the specialist is at greater risk in exactly the disturbed landscapes a conservation report is usually written about.',
