          prompt: 'Explain why a sprinter’s muscles rely heavily on anaerobic respiration during a very short, intense sprint, despite anaerobic respiration producing far less ATP per glucose molecule than aerobic respiration.',
          answer:
            'During a very short, extremely intense sprint, the sprinter’s muscles require ATP at an extremely high rate, far faster than the circulatory and respiratory systems can deliver oxygen to the muscle cells to sustain fully aerobic respiration at the required rate. Anaerobic respiration, although far less efficient in terms of ATP produced per glucose molecule, can produce that (smaller) amount of ATP extremely rapidly, without needing to wait for an adequate oxygen supply, allowing the muscles to generate ATP quickly enough to sustain the very high-intensity, short-duration effort of a sprint, even though this comes at the cost of producing lactic acid and being unsustainable for more than a short period.',
          explanation: 'This illustrates a genuine physiological trade-off: anaerobic respiration sacrifices overall efficiency (ATP yield per glucose) for a much faster maximum rate of ATP production, which is precisely what is needed for very short, extremely intense bursts of activity, unlike aerobic respiration, which is more efficient but cannot ramp up its ATP production rate as quickly.',
