          answer: 'Oxygen debt refers to the extra volume of oxygen the body must consume after a period of vigorous exercise, above what it would normally use at rest, in order to fully recover from the effects of anaerobic respiration that occurred during that exercise. After vigorous exercise ends, breathing and heart rate remain elevated for some time, supplying this additional oxygen, which is used to break down (oxidise) the lactic acid that accumulated in the muscles during anaerobic respiration, converting it back into other useful substances (or ultimately breaking it down fully via aerobic respiration), and to replenish other depleted energy reserves.',
          explanation: 'This is why a person continues breathing heavily and has an elevated heart rate for some time after stopping intense exercise, even though the exercise itself has ended — the body is actively "repaying" this accumulated oxygen debt from the preceding period of anaerobic respiration.',
