          answer: 'Mucus-secreting (goblet) cells produce a sticky layer of mucus that traps inhaled dust particles, bacteria and other debris before they can reach the delicate alveoli deeper in the lungs. The cilia (tiny hair-like projections) lining these same airways beat in a coordinated, wave-like manner, continuously sweeping this mucus (along with the trapped debris) upward, away from the lungs and toward the throat, where it can be swallowed or coughed out.',
          explanation: 'This combined mucus-and-cilia system, sometimes called the mucociliary escalator, provides an important first line of defence, continuously clearing potentially harmful inhaled particles and microorganisms from the airways before they can reach and damage the delicate gas-exchange surfaces of the alveoli.',
