          context: 'Biologists disagree about viruses. One position is that viruses should be given a kingdom of their own within the classification system. Another is that they should be left out of it entirely, because they are not living.',
          prompt: 'Which position does the evidence better support? Justify your answer by referring to the criteria on which the five-kingdom system classifies organisms.',
          answer: 'Either position can be argued, and what is assessed is whether the criteria are used. The five-kingdom system sorts organisms by features of their cells: whether the cell is prokaryotic or eukaryotic, whether the organism is unicellular or multicellular, and how it obtains its nutrition. Viruses answer none of these questions, because they are not made of cells at all -- they are nucleic acid in a protein coat, with no cytoplasm, no organelles and no membrane-bound nucleus. They also carry out none of the life processes independently: they do not respire, grow, excrete or metabolise, and they can only replicate by taking over a host cell\'s machinery. On these grounds the exclusion argument is strong, because a system built on cell structure has no criterion that a non-cellular entity could be measured against, and adding a kingdom for something the criteria cannot describe weakens the system rather than extending it. The counter-argument is that viruses do possess genetic material, do evolve by mutation and natural selection, and are unquestionably biological, so leaving them out of biological classification is an admission that the system is too narrow rather than a statement about viruses. A good answer commits to one position and shows why the criteria support it; a weak answer lists properties of viruses without connecting them to what the system actually classifies on.',
