          context: 'A patient is prescribed a seven-day course of antibiotics. After three days the symptoms are gone and the patient feels well. A friend advises stopping, saying that taking medicine you no longer need is wasteful and that the leftover tablets can be saved for the next infection.',
          prompt: 'Evaluate both parts of the friend\'s advice, and explain what the consequence of following it would be for the patient and for other people.',
          answer: 'Both parts are wrong, and for related reasons. On stopping early: the bacteria causing an infection vary in how susceptible they are to the antibiotic, and the most susceptible die first. Symptoms disappear once the population has fallen enough for the immune system to cope, but the survivors at that point are disproportionately the least susceptible ones. Stopping then leaves precisely those bacteria alive, and the course is designed to run long enough to kill them too. Feeling better is therefore evidence that the drug is working, not evidence that it has finished -- which is the friend\'s central mistake. The surviving resistant bacteria may multiply and cause a relapse that the same antibiotic no longer clears, and because bacteria can pass resistance genes to one another, resistance can spread beyond this patient to strains infecting other people, which is how the harm stops being private. On saving the leftovers: self-prescribing later means an unknown dose, for an unidentified organism, probably for an infection this drug does not treat -- and, critically, an incomplete course by definition, which repeats the same selection for resistance a second time. What the friend has correctly noticed is that unnecessary antibiotic use is genuinely harmful; the conclusion drawn from it is the opposite of the right one.',
