          marks: 4,
          context: 'A timber company proposes to clear-fell a hillside plantation and replant immediately with seedlings, arguing that because the same number of trees will be growing within a year, there will be no lasting environmental harm.',
          prompt: 'Do you agree that replanting immediately prevents lasting harm? Justify your answer by referring to soil erosion.',
          answer: 'No -- the argument counts the trees and ignores the soil, which is what is actually lost. Clear-felling removes the whole canopy at once, so rain strikes bare ground directly instead of being intercepted by leaves, and it removes the root systems that bind soil particles together and hold the slope in place. The roots of felled trees rot over the months that follow, so the soil is least stable in exactly the period before the seedlings have established. On a hillside, where water runs off fastest, this is when the topsoil is stripped -- and topsoil is the layer holding the organic matter and nutrients, built over decades and not replaced in a year. Seedlings planted into what remains grow more slowly in poorer, thinner soil, so the recovery the company promises is itself undermined by the damage. The eroded material also does not simply vanish: it is carried into streams, where it raises turbidity, smothers riverbed habitats and can silt up dams downstream, so the harm reaches beyond the hillside. The company\'s claim would be stronger if it proposed felling in strips or patches, leaving intact root networks between them to hold the slope while the new trees establish.',
