          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          cognitiveLevel: 4,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A municipality can afford one of two upgrades to a failing sewage works discharging into a river. Upgrade A removes most of the remaining organic matter from the effluent. Upgrade B removes most of the nitrates and phosphates. A councillor argues for A, on the grounds that the organic matter is what smells and what visibly fouls the water.',
          prompt: 'Which upgrade would you recommend, and why? Justify your recommendation by referring to how eutrophication lowers dissolved oxygen in the river.',
          answer: 'Upgrade B is the better recommendation, although the councillor has correctly identified the more visible problem. Untreated sewage lowers river oxygen by two routes, and they are not equal in scale. The organic matter is decomposed by bacteria which consume dissolved oxygen as they do so, and this depletes oxygen near the discharge point -- unpleasant, visible, and largely local. The nitrates and phosphates do something different: they act as fertiliser for algae and aquatic plants, which multiply into a dense bloom that shades out the plants beneath, and when that large mass of algae dies it is decomposed by bacteria whose respiration consumes dissolved oxygen on a far larger scale and over a far longer stretch of river. Fish and invertebrates then suffocate. So the organic matter causes the damage the councillor can see, while the nutrients cause the damage that kills the river, and the nutrient route also amplifies itself, since each bloom dies and feeds the next round of decomposition. Removing the nutrients addresses the cause of the larger oxygen sag; removing the organic matter cleans up the symptom nearest the pipe. The councillor\'s case is not empty -- if the works discharges into a slow pool used by a community for washing, the immediate local oxygen crash and the smell may be the more urgent harm -- but on the health of the river as a whole, B is the defensible choice.',
          explanation: 'Both options are genuinely beneficial, so this cannot be answered by spotting the wrong one. The marks are for comparing the scale and the persistence of two mechanisms.',
