          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 6,
          context: 'A farmer plants a field of maize two years running and the second crop is noticeably poorer. A neighbour alternates maize with beans, and both crops do well every year. Bean roots carry small swellings; maize roots do not. The air is about 78% nitrogen.',
          prompt: 'Explain why the maize-only field declines although the air above it is mostly nitrogen, and explain what the swellings on the bean roots have to do with the neighbour’s better result.',
          answer: 'Plants cannot use nitrogen gas. The two nitrogen atoms in N₂ are held by an exceptionally strong triple bond, and no plant has the means to break it, so the nitrogen in the air is unavailable to a maize crop however much of it there is. Plants take up nitrogen only as nitrates dissolved in soil water, and a maize crop removes nitrates from the soil and is then harvested and carried away, so the nitrogen it contains never returns to that field. Grown twice in succession, the crop depletes the soil nitrate and the second harvest is poorer. The swellings on the bean roots are nodules containing Rhizobium bacteria, which live in a mutualistic relationship with the plant. These bacteria can carry out nitrogen fixation: they possess the enzyme needed to break the triple bond and convert nitrogen gas into ammonium compounds the plant can use. The bean supplies them with sugars and a protected site; they supply the bean with usable nitrogen. Because the beans obtain their nitrogen from the air rather than from the soil, and because nitrogen-rich root material is left behind when the crop is lifted, the soil is not depleted and is partly replenished. Alternating the crops therefore lets the field recover between maize plantings.',
          explanation: 'The puzzle is that a field can be nitrogen-starved under an atmosphere that is mostly nitrogen, and the resolution is chemical: the triple bond in N₂ makes it inert to plants. The nodules are the answer because they house organisms that CAN break it, which is why legumes are used in rotation and why this piece of biology has been farming practice for centuries.',
