          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Describe the structure of a phospholipid molecule, and explain how this structure differs from that of a triglyceride.',
          answer: 'A phospholipid is made up of a glycerol molecule bonded to two fatty acid chains (rather than three) and a phosphate group, which occupies the position of the third fatty acid found in a triglyceride. This gives a phospholipid a hydrophilic (water-attracting) phosphate ‘head’ and two hydrophobic (water-repelling) fatty acid ‘tails’, unlike a triglyceride, which consists of three fatty acid chains attached to glycerol and is entirely hydrophobic overall.',
          explanation: 'This dual water-attracting/water-repelling structure is precisely what allows phospholipids to spontaneously arrange themselves into the bilayer that forms the basic structure of a cell membrane, with their hydrophilic heads facing the watery environment on each side and their hydrophobic tails facing one another in the middle.',
