          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A plant cell is placed in pure water and swells until it is firm, but does not burst. An animal cell placed in pure water swells and bursts. A plant cell placed in concentrated salt solution shrinks away from its wall, but the wall keeps its shape.',
          prompt: 'Explain the different roles of the cell wall and the cell membrane, and use them to account for all three observations.',
          answer: 'The cell membrane is a thin, flexible, partially permeable layer that controls which substances enter and leave the cell; water crosses it freely by osmosis, so it cannot prevent water entering. The cell wall is a thick, rigid layer of cellulose outside the membrane. It is fully permeable, so it controls nothing about what enters, and its role is mechanical: it resists stretching and gives the cell its shape and support. In pure water, water enters both cells by osmosis because the cell contents are more concentrated than the water outside. The animal cell has only its membrane to contain the swelling, and the membrane stretches until it bursts. The plant cell swells too, but the wall cannot stretch, so as the contents press outwards the wall pushes back and pressure builds inside until it stops any further water entering. The cell becomes firm — turgid — and survives. In concentrated salt solution the water potential outside is lower than inside, so water leaves the plant cell by osmosis. The contents shrink and the membrane pulls away from the wall, but the wall is rigid and holds its shape regardless, which is why a plant wilts and becomes floppy rather than shrivelling.',
          explanation: 'Keep the two jobs separate — the membrane controls ENTRY, the wall provides SUPPORT — and each observation follows without further facts. The most informative case is the plant cell in pure water: the wall saves it not by keeping water out, which it cannot do, but by generating a back-pressure that stops the osmosis, which is also the pressure that holds a non-woody plant upright.',
