          prompt: 'Explain why interphase, rather than mitosis itself, occupies the greatest proportion of time in a typical cell cycle.',
          answer:
            'Most of a cell’s essential preparatory activities take place during interphase, including cell growth, the duplication of organelles, ongoing protein synthesis, and — most critically — the accurate replication of the cell’s entire DNA content, so that every chromosome is duplicated into two identical sister chromatids before division begins. Carrying out and carefully checking this large amount of preparation, especially the precise copying of the whole genome, takes considerably longer than the comparatively brief, largely mechanical process of physically separating the already-duplicated chromosomes during the mitosis stage itself.',
          explanation: 'This is why interphase is sometimes mistakenly thought of as a resting stage, when in fact it is the longest and most metabolically active part of the entire cell cycle.',
