          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A lizard loses its tail to a predator and regrows a new one over several months. A person cuts their finger and the wound closes over within two weeks. Both new tissues are genetically identical to the tissue around them.',
          prompt: 'Explain what mitosis contributes in each case, and explain why the new tissue is genetically identical to the old rather than merely similar.',
          answer: 'In both cases mitosis supplies new cells. In the lizard it replaces a whole lost structure: cells at the stump divide repeatedly to produce the large number of cells needed to rebuild muscle, skin, connective tissue and supporting rod, which is why the process takes months. In the healing finger it repairs damage on a smaller scale: cells at the edges of the wound divide and the new cells spread across the gap until the surfaces meet and the skin is continuous again, which takes days rather than months because far fewer cells are needed. The same process therefore serves growth, repair and replacement, differing only in how much is required. The new tissue is genetically identical, not merely similar, because of what mitosis does with the DNA. Before division the cell replicates every chromosome exactly, producing two identical copies joined together. During mitosis these copies are separated so that one goes to each daughter cell, which means each daughter receives a complete and identical set of chromosomes — the same number and the same alleles as the parent cell. Since every cell in the new tail or the new skin descends by mitosis from cells of the original body, all of them carry the same genetic information, and the repair is a continuation of the same individual rather than new tissue in any genetic sense.',
          explanation: 'Growth, repair and replacement are one process at different scales, and saying so is better than treating them as three facts. The genetic half rests on replication coming first: the copying is exact and mitosis merely separates the copies, which is why daughter cells are identical — and it is the same reason a cutting from a plant grows into a clone of its parent.',
