          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Explain why a chromosome must be duplicated into two identical sister chromatids during interphase, before mitosis can produce two daughter cells with a complete set of chromosomes.',
          answer: 'Mitosis produces two separate daughter cells, and each of these daughter cells must receive a complete set of chromosomes, identical in number and content to the set found in the original parent cell. Since only one copy of each chromosome is present at the start of interphase, this single copy must first be duplicated (replicated) into two identical sister chromatids before division begins, so that, once the sister chromatids are separated during anaphase, one complete copy of each chromosome is available to be allocated to each of the two daughter cells, without either daughter cell ending up with less genetic material than the original parent cell had.',
