          answer: 'Sieve tube elements lose most of their organelles, including their nucleus, as they mature, in order to maximise the internal space available for transporting dissolved sugars, which means they are no longer able to independently carry out all the metabolic functions a living cell normally requires. Companion cells, which are connected to their associated sieve tube elements by numerous cytoplasmic connections (plasmodesmata), retain a full set of organelles and carry out these essential metabolic functions on the sieve tube element’s behalf, including generating the ATP needed to actively load and unload sugars into and out of the phloem.',
