          prompt: 'Explain how the arrangement of the palisade and spongy mesophyll layers within a leaf maximises its photosynthetic efficiency, and explain the adaptive significance of most stomata being located on the lower epidermis rather than the upper epidermis.',
          answer:
            'The palisade mesophyll consists of tightly packed, column-shaped cells positioned just below the upper epidermis, each containing many chloroplasts, which maximises the absorption of incoming sunlight near the leaf’s upper surface, where light intensity is greatest. The spongy mesophyll, below the palisade layer, consists of loosely arranged, irregularly shaped cells with large interconnected air spaces, which allow carbon dioxide and oxygen to diffuse easily between the mesophyll cells and the stomata. Most stomata are located on the lower epidermis, which generally receives less direct sunlight and heat than the upper surface, so the air immediately around these lower-surface stomata tends to remain relatively cooler and more humid, reducing the rate of water loss by transpiration compared with placing an equal number of stomata on the hotter, more exposed upper surface.',
