          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain why the flow of energy through an ecosystem is described as ‘unidirectional’ (one-way), and explain how the movement of nutrients through the same ecosystem differs from this.',
          answer:
            'Energy enters an ecosystem as light energy from the sun, captured by producers during photosynthesis and converted into chemical energy stored within organic molecules. As this chemical energy passes from one trophic level to the next, a large proportion of it is lost at each transfer as heat, a by-product of cellular respiration, and this lost heat energy can never be recaptured or reused by any other organism in the ecosystem. Because this energy is permanently lost rather than being recycled, energy is described as flowing through an ecosystem in one direction only, meaning an ecosystem must continuously receive a fresh supply of solar energy to be sustained. Nutrients, such as carbon and nitrogen, are not lost from an ecosystem in this same way; instead, the same nutrient atoms are repeatedly recycled between organisms and the physical environment, through processes such as photosynthesis, respiration and decomposition, so nutrient movement is described as cyclical rather than one-way.',
          explanation: 'This fundamental difference explains why an ecosystem requires a constant, ongoing input of solar energy to persist, whereas the same finite pool of carbon or nitrogen atoms can, in principle, be reused indefinitely within the ecosystem.',
