          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A sample of nucleic acid is analysed. It contains ribose, it contains uracil but no thymine, and it is single-stranded. A second sample contains deoxyribose, thymine but no uracil, and is double-stranded.',
          prompt: 'Identify each sample, giving your reasons, and explain why the molecule that stores genetic information permanently is the double-stranded one rather than the single-stranded one.',
          answer: 'The first sample is RNA: ribose as its sugar, uracil in place of thymine, and a single strand are all characteristic of it. The second is DNA: deoxyribose, thymine and a double strand. Being double-stranded suits DNA to permanent storage for two reasons. The first is stability. The two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between paired bases all along their length, and the bases are turned inwards, protected within the helix, while the sugar-phosphate backbones face outwards. That makes the molecule far more stable and far less easily damaged than a single strand with its bases exposed, which matters for information that must last a lifetime and be passed on. The second is the copying, and it is the more important. Because the strands are complementary, each one carries all the information needed to rebuild the other. The molecule can be unzipped and each strand used as a template to assemble a new partner, producing two identical copies — which is exactly what replication is, and what makes it accurate. A single strand has no such backup, so a damaged region cannot be restored by reference to a partner. RNA has no need of either property, because it is made as required, used, and broken down again.',
