          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A botanist is given three unlabelled plant specimens. Specimen 1 has broad thin leaves with pores on both surfaces. Specimen 2 has thick fleshy leaves with a heavy waxy coating, few pores, and those sunk into pits lined with hairs. Specimen 3 has narrow rolled leaves with the pores on the inner surface of the roll.',
          prompt: 'Identify which specimens are adapted to dry conditions, and explain how THREE of the features described reduce water loss.',
          answer: 'Specimens 2 and 3 are adapted to dry conditions; specimen 1 is not, since broad thin leaves with pores on both surfaces maximise both light capture and water loss and suit a plant with water to spare. Taking three of the dry-adapted features: the heavy waxy coating is waterproof, so water cannot evaporate through the leaf surface itself and is lost only through the pores, which the plant can control. Having few pores reduces the total area through which water vapour can escape, at the cost of admitting less carbon dioxide. Sinking the pores into hair-lined pits traps a pocket of still, humid air immediately outside each pore; because the air there is already moist, the difference in water vapour concentration between the inside of the leaf and the air just outside it is small, so the gradient driving evaporation is shallow and water leaves far more slowly. Rolling the leaf with the pores on the inside does the same thing on a larger scale, enclosing the pores in a humid chamber and sheltering them from wind that would otherwise carry the moist air away and keep the gradient steep. Every one of these features works either by blocking the route or by reducing the gradient.',
