          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'Two leaves. Leaf A is long and narrow, with veins running parallel from base to tip. Leaf B is broad, with one main vein from which smaller veins branch repeatedly into a network. Their seedlings were also examined: one had a single first leaf, the other two.',
          prompt: 'Match each leaf to the number of first leaves its seedling had, giving your reason, and explain what else this tells the botanist about the arrangement of tissues inside each plant’s stem.',
          answer: 'Leaf A, with parallel veins, belongs to the seedling with one first leaf: parallel venation is characteristic of monocotyledons, which have a single cotyledon. Leaf B, with branching net venation, belongs to the seedling with two: net venation is characteristic of dicotyledons, which have two cotyledons. The value of the match is that these features occur together reliably, so identifying one tells you the others. That extends to the stem. A monocotyledon such as leaf A’s plant has its vascular bundles scattered throughout the stem tissue, with no cambium between the xylem and phloem, so it cannot form a continuous cambial cylinder and cannot thicken by secondary growth — which is why monocots such as grasses stay relatively slender. A dicotyledon such as leaf B’s plant has its bundles arranged in a ring near the outside of the stem, each with a cambium between xylem and phloem, and that cambium can form a continuous ring, dividing to add new xylem inwards year after year. That is why dicots can become trees. From a single leaf, therefore, the botanist can predict the cotyledon number, the stem anatomy and whether the plant is capable of becoming woody.',
          explanation: 'The point is that these characters are CORRELATED, not independent: venation, cotyledon number, root structure, flower parts and stem anatomy all sort the same way, which is what makes the monocot–dicot division useful rather than arbitrary. The stem consequence is the most substantial, since the presence or absence of a continuous cambium decides whether a plant can ever become a tree.',
