          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A plant is well watered in the morning and its stomata are open. By midday, after several hours of hot dry wind, the stomata have closed although the sun is still bright and the plant is still photosynthesising.',
          prompt: 'Explain how guard cells open a stoma, in terms of osmosis and turgor, and explain why closing the stomata at midday is worth the cost to the plant.',
          answer: 'A stoma is a pore between two guard cells. To open it, the guard cells actively take up potassium ions, which lowers the water potential of the cell sap, so water follows by osmosis from the surrounding cells. The guard cells become turgid, and because their inner walls facing the pore are thicker and less elastic than their outer walls, they cannot swell evenly: the thin outer wall stretches more, bowing each cell outwards and pulling the thick inner walls apart, which opens the pore. To close it the process reverses — ions leave, water follows out, the cells lose turgor and become flaccid, and the walls straighten so the pore closes. Closing at midday is worth the cost because the plant is losing water faster than it can replace it. Hot dry wind greatly increases evaporation from the open stomata, and if that continues the cells throughout the plant lose turgor, it wilts, and eventually its cells are damaged beyond recovery. Closing the stomata stops the loss almost entirely. The cost is real: carbon dioxide can no longer enter, so photosynthesis slows sharply and the plant makes less food that afternoon. But a lost afternoon of growth can be made up and a dead plant cannot, so the plant sacrifices food production to protect its water. It is choosing survival over growth.',
          explanation: 'The unequal thickness of the guard cell walls is what turns swelling into opening, and an answer that has the cells swell without explaining why the pore opens has missed the mechanism. The midday closure is the same trade-off that shapes the whole leaf: carbon dioxide in against water out, and when water becomes the scarcer resource the plant shuts the door and accepts the loss.',
