          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain why root hair cells, with their large surface-area-to-volume ratio, are well adapted for absorbing water and mineral ions from the soil.',
          answer: 'Each root hair cell has a long, thin, tubular extension (the root hair) projecting out into the soil, greatly increasing the surface area of the cell in contact with the surrounding soil water, without significantly increasing the cell’s volume. This large surface-area-to-volume ratio maximises the area available for water to enter by osmosis and for mineral ions to be absorbed (partly by diffusion, and partly by active transport against their concentration gradient), allowing the plant to absorb these substances much more efficiently than a cell without such an extension could.',
          explanation: 'The huge number of individual root hairs on a root system further multiplies this total absorptive surface area, which is why even a relatively small root system can supply an entire plant with sufficient water and minerals.',
