          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Describe the structure and function of cartilage, and explain why damaged cartilage heals only very slowly.',
          answer: 'Cartilage is a firm but flexible connective tissue, in which cells (chondrocytes) are embedded within a dense matrix rich in collagen and elastic fibres. It provides flexible support and cushioning, for example in the outer ear, the trachea, and at the ends of bones within joints. Cartilage lacks its own direct blood supply, so nutrients and oxygen must diffuse to its cells slowly from surrounding tissue, and waste products must diffuse away in the same slow manner; this very limited nutrient and oxygen supply is why damaged cartilage heals only very slowly, compared to tissues such as bone that do have a direct blood supply.',
          explanation: 'This slow healing is why cartilage injuries, such as a torn meniscus in the knee, often take a long time to recover, or may require surgical intervention.',
