          topicId: 'life-sci-animal-tissues',
          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain why skeletal muscle tissue eventually becomes fatigued during sustained, intense exercise.',
          answer: 'During sustained, intense exercise, the demand for energy in skeletal muscle cells can exceed the rate at which oxygen can be delivered to them, forcing the muscle cells to rely increasingly on anaerobic respiration to supplement their energy production. Anaerobic respiration is far less efficient at releasing energy than aerobic respiration, and produces lactic acid as a by-product, which builds up within the muscle tissue and interferes with muscle contraction. In addition, the muscle’s stored glycogen (its main fuel reserve) becomes progressively depleted the longer the exercise continues. The combined effect of a lactic acid build-up and depleted energy reserves is what is experienced as muscle fatigue.',
          explanation: 'This is why an athlete can only sustain very intense effort for a short time before needing to slow down or rest, allowing the body to clear the built-up lactic acid and replenish glycogen and oxygen levels.',
