          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A deciduous fruit tree was studied through the year. In early spring, before any leaves had opened, the buds swelled and grew rapidly. In midsummer the tree was in full leaf and its fruit was swelling. In autumn the leaves yellowed and fell, and the roots gained mass.',
          prompt: 'Explain what is meant by a source and a sink in translocation, and use the three seasons to explain why the direction of flow in the phloem changes during the year.',
          answer: 'A source is any part of a plant that releases sugar into the phloem — usually a part that is photosynthesising, or one releasing sugar from storage. A sink is any part that removes sugar from the phloem, either to use it in respiration and growth or to store it. Sugar is translocated from source to sink, so the direction of flow depends entirely on which parts are which at that moment, and the same organ can be one at one time of year and the other at another. In early spring there are no leaves, so nothing is photosynthesising; the sugar must come from stores laid down in the roots and stem, which act as the source, and the growing buds are the sink. Flow is therefore upwards. In midsummer the leaves are the source, since they are photosynthesising and producing a surplus, and the swelling fruit and the growing roots are sinks, so flow is largely downwards and outwards to them. In autumn the leaves yellow and are shut down, their remaining sugars are withdrawn before they fall, and the roots gain mass because they are now storing for the winter — so the leaves are a source for the last time and the roots are the sink. The same roots that supplied the buds in spring are receiving in autumn, which is exactly what source and sink mean.',
          explanation: 'Source and sink are ROLES rather than places, and the tree is the proof: the roots are the source in spring and the sink in autumn without changing at all. That reversal is the difference between translocation in the phloem and the transpiration stream in the xylem, which only ever runs one way.',
