          context: 'Bone is living tissue in which some cells continuously remove old bone and others lay down new bone. From about the age of 35 the removal begins to outpace the replacement. Bone scans of an 80-year-old show the same outline as at 30, but the internal spongy bone has far fewer and thinner struts.',
          prompt: 'Explain why an elderly person’s bones break more easily, referring to what the scans show, and explain why exercise is recommended to reduce the risk.',
          answer: 'Bone is not a fixed structure but one in constant turnover, with cells removing and replacing it throughout life. While the two are in balance, bone mass stays constant; once removal outpaces replacement, bone mass declines year by year. The scans show where the loss occurs: the outline is unchanged, so the bone is the same size and appears normal from outside, but the spongy bone inside has fewer and thinner struts. Those struts carry and spread the load, so losing them means the same force is borne by less material and the bone fractures under a stress it would once have withstood. This is why a fall that a young person shrugs off can break an elderly person’s hip, and why the weakening is invisible until a fracture reveals it. Exercise helps because the rate at which new bone is laid down responds to the forces put through it. Bone that is regularly loaded — by walking, lifting or any weight-bearing activity — receives a signal to build, so the bone-forming cells work harder and the balance shifts back towards replacement. Inactivity does the reverse: bone that is not loaded is not maintained, which is why prolonged bed rest and weightlessness both cause rapid bone loss. Exercise does not stop the ageing process but slows the net loss.',
          explanation: 'The turnover framing is what makes this explicable: the bones are not wearing out passively, the removal side of a balance has come to exceed the building side. The scan detail matters because it locates the loss internally, which is why the weakening is hidden — and the exercise answer follows from bone responding to LOAD, which is also why astronauts lose bone despite being young and healthy.',
