          answer: 'Decomposers (such as bacteria and fungi) obtain energy by breaking down the complex organic compounds present in dead organisms and organic waste, releasing simpler substances back into the environment. In doing so, decomposers act as the final recipients of energy at the end of a food chain, extracting the last remaining chemical energy from organic matter that would otherwise no longer be available to any other organism.',
          explanation: 'Because all organisms in a food chain eventually die (or produce waste), decomposers play an essential role in extracting the final usable energy from organic matter, before that matter’s remaining nutrients are recycled back into the ecosystem.',
