          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          cognitiveLevel: 1,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Describe what happens during metaphase of mitosis.',
          answer: 'During metaphase, the chromosomes (each consisting of two sister chromatids, joined at the centromere) become attached to spindle fibres at their centromeres, and are moved so that they line up individually along the equator (the metaphase plate) of the cell, midway between the two poles.',
          explanation: 'This precise alignment along the equator ensures that, when the sister chromatids are later separated during anaphase, each set moves an equal distance to its respective pole.',
