          answer: 'Sucrose produced during photosynthesis is actively transported (using energy) from the photosynthesising cells of the leaf into the phloem sieve tubes, with the help of companion cells that assist this active loading process. This active loading raises the sucrose concentration within the sieve tubes, lowering their water potential relative to surrounding cells, which causes water to also move into the sieve tubes by osmosis, generating the pressure that helps drive translocation of the sucrose solution to other parts of the plant.',
