          answer: 'The right ventricle only needs to pump blood a relatively short distance, to the nearby lungs (as part of pulmonary circulation), so it generates a relatively lower pressure and requires a thinner muscular wall. The left ventricle, by contrast, must pump blood all the way around the rest of the body (as part of systemic circulation), a much greater distance against greater overall resistance, so it must generate a much higher pressure, requiring a considerably thicker, more powerful muscular wall to produce this greater force.',
          explanation: 'This difference in wall thickness directly reflects the different pressures each ventricle must generate to successfully pump blood through its respective circuit.',
