          topicId: 'life-sci-chemistry-of-life',
          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'Twenty different amino acids are used to build proteins. A short chain of just three amino acids can be assembled in 20 × 20 × 20 different orders. Sickle cell anaemia is caused by a single amino acid being substituted at one position in a chain of 146.',
          prompt: 'Calculate how many different three-amino-acid chains are possible, and use the sickle cell example to explain why the ORDER of amino acids matters so much, not just which ones are present.',
          answer: '20 × 20 × 20 = 8 000 possible chains of three amino acids, and a chain of any useful length gives a number far beyond counting. Amino acids join when the acid group of one reacts with the amino group of the next, forming a peptide bond and releasing water, and because any amino acid can follow any other the sequence is entirely free — which is what allows so many different proteins. The order matters because the sequence determines how the chain folds. Each amino acid has its own side group with its own chemical properties, and as the chain is built these side groups attract or repel one another and form bonds between particular positions, pulling the chain into one specific three-dimensional shape. A protein works by its shape: an enzyme by the shape of its active site, haemoglobin by a shape that binds oxygen. Change one amino acid and the side group at that position changes, so the bonds it forms change, and the fold can change with it. In sickle cell anaemia a single substitution in 146 creates a sticky patch on the surface of haemoglobin, so the molecules join into long fibres that distort the whole red blood cell. One position out of 146 is enough to change the shape, and changing the shape changes what the protein does.',
          explanation: 'Do the multiplication to see how large the space of possible proteins is, then use the sickle cell case to see how narrow the space of WORKING ones can be. The link to hold on to is sequence → folding → shape → function: the order matters because it is the order that decides the fold, which is why a single substitution among 146 can be the difference between health and disease.',
