          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain how a poison that binds permanently to an enzyme and distorts the shape of its active site would affect the enzyme’s ability to catalyse its normal reaction, and explain why simply increasing the substrate concentration would NOT restore normal activity.',
          answer:
            'If a poison binds to an enzyme and permanently distorts the shape of its active site, the active site is no longer complementary in shape to its normal substrate, so the substrate can no longer bind to it effectively (or at all), and the enzyme loses its ability to catalyse its normal reaction. Increasing the substrate concentration would not restore this activity, because the problem lies with the altered shape of the enzyme’s active site itself, not a shortage of substrate molecules — no matter how much substrate is made available, it simply cannot bind to an active site whose shape no longer matches it.',
          explanation: 'This reinforces that enzyme activity depends on a precise, complementary fit between an active site and its substrate; once that fit is permanently destroyed, no amount of additional substrate can compensate for the loss.',
