          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A root hair cell is a long narrow extension of an epidermal cell, up to 1 mm long and a few micrometres wide. It has a thin cell wall, no chloroplasts, a large vacuole containing concentrated cell sap, and unusually many mitochondria.',
          prompt: 'Explain how THREE of these features suit the cell to absorbing water and mineral salts, and explain why it has no chloroplasts but a great many mitochondria.',
          answer: 'The long narrow shape gives a very large surface area in contact with the soil water for a very small volume of cell, and since the rate of absorption depends on the area available, this is what allows one cell to take up so much. The thin cell wall means water and dissolved salts have only a short distance to cross to reach the cell membrane, and since rate of movement falls as distance rises, a thin wall speeds uptake. The large vacuole of concentrated cell sap keeps the solution inside the cell more concentrated than the soil water outside, maintaining the water potential gradient that drives water in by osmosis — without it, water would stop entering as soon as the concentrations equalised. The absence of chloroplasts and the abundance of mitochondria both follow from where the cell lives and what it does. It is underground in the dark, so chloroplasts would be useless and building them would be a waste of materials. But mineral salts are usually more concentrated inside the root than in the soil, so taking them up means moving them against their gradient by active transport, which needs a continuous supply of ATP. Many mitochondria supply it. The cell is therefore stripped of what it cannot use and stocked with what its particular work demands.',
          explanation: 'Each feature should be tied to a rate — surface area, distance, gradient — since those are the three things that govern how fast anything crosses a membrane. The organelles are the revealing part: what a cell LACKS is as informative as what it has, and a root hair cell with no chloroplasts and many mitochondria tells you it lives in the dark and does a great deal of active transport.',
