          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'Two stems in cross-section. Stem A has vascular bundles scattered throughout the tissue. Stem B has its vascular bundles arranged in a ring near the outside, with a cambium between the xylem and phloem of each bundle. Stem B belongs to a tree; stem A belongs to a grass.',
          prompt: 'Identify which stem is monocotyledonous and which dicotyledonous, and explain how the ring arrangement in stem B gives an advantage that the scattered arrangement does not.',
          answer: 'Stem A, with scattered bundles, is monocotyledonous, which fits its being a grass. Stem B, with bundles in a ring and a cambium, is dicotyledonous, which fits its being a tree. The ring arrangement gives two advantages. The first is mechanical. When a stem bends, the greatest stress is at its outer edges, so placing the strong, thick-walled xylem tissue in a ring near the outside puts the strengthening material where it resists bending most effectively — the same principle that makes a hollow tube stiffer than a solid rod of the same weight. The second is growth. The cambium between xylem and phloem in each bundle is a lateral meristem, and because the bundles lie in a ring the cambium can form a continuous cylinder right round the stem. Dividing all round, it adds new xylem inwards and new phloem outwards year after year, so the stem thickens — this is secondary growth, and it is what allows a dicot to become a tree and support great height and weight. Scattered bundles cannot form a continuous cambial cylinder, so a monocot cannot thicken in this way, which is why grasses and most monocots stay comparatively slender and rely on other means of support.',
          explanation: 'Identify from the arrangement first, then give the ring two separate credits, because they are genuinely different arguments: one about where strength is needed in a bending stem, one about what a continuous cambium makes possible. The second is the more far-reaching, since the ability to thicken indefinitely is why trees are dicots.',
