          context: 'An enzyme extracted from a hot spring bacterium still works at 85 °C. The same enzyme from a related bacterium living in cool soil stops working above about 45 °C. Their amino acid sequences differ at only a few positions.',
          prompt: 'Explain why a change in an enzyme’s shape stops it working, and explain how a difference at only a few positions in the amino acid sequence can produce such a large difference in heat tolerance.',
          answer: 'An enzyme works because its active site has a precise three-dimensional shape that fits the shape of its substrate. Only a molecule with the matching shape can enter and be held in place, which is what makes an enzyme specific to one reaction. High temperature makes the molecule vibrate strongly enough to break the weak bonds holding the folded chain in position, so the protein loses its shape and the active site is no longer the right shape for the substrate. The substrate can no longer bind, and the enzyme is denatured and cannot be repaired by cooling it again. A few amino acid changes matter so much because the three-dimensional shape is held by bonds between particular amino acids at particular positions along the chain. If a substitution creates extra bonds — or stronger ones — between parts of the folded chain, the whole structure resists being shaken apart and holds its shape to a much higher temperature. Nothing needs to change at the active site itself: the substitutions can be elsewhere in the molecule and still stabilise the fold that holds the active site in the correct shape. So a small change in sequence can produce a large change in stability, because sequence determines folding and folding determines both shape and how firmly that shape is held.',
