          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'Glucose uptake into a cell was measured against the glucose concentration outside it. As the outside concentration rises, uptake rises steeply at first, then the increase slows, and above a certain concentration uptake stops rising however much more glucose is added. No ATP is used.',
          prompt: 'Explain why this pattern shows facilitated diffusion rather than simple diffusion, and explain why uptake levels off at high concentrations.',
          answer: 'In simple diffusion, molecules pass directly through the phospholipid bilayer, so the rate depends only on the steepness of the concentration gradient. Doubling the outside concentration doubles the gradient and doubles the rate, and the graph is a straight line that never levels off. That is not what happens here. Facilitated diffusion uses transport proteins in the membrane, which glucose is too large and too water-soluble to cross without. Like simple diffusion it moves substances down their concentration gradient and uses no ATP, which is why no energy is consumed here, but unlike simple diffusion it depends on a limited number of protein molecules. That limit is what produces the levelling off. At low concentrations most transport proteins are free at any moment, so raising the concentration outside means more glucose molecules meet an available protein and the rate rises steeply. As the concentration rises further, more and more of the proteins are occupied, so each extra increase brings a smaller gain. Above a certain point every transport protein is working continuously, and the rate is set by how fast the proteins can cycle rather than by how much glucose is present. Adding more glucose then makes no difference, and the curve becomes flat: the membrane is saturated.',
