          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Describe the structure and function of tendons and ligaments, and state ONE difference between them.',
          answer: 'Tendons and ligaments are both dense connective tissues made up mainly of tough, non-elastic collagen fibres, arranged in the direction of the force or tension they experience. Tendons attach muscle to bone, transmitting the force of a muscle’s contraction to move the bone; ligaments attach bone to bone across a joint, helping to hold the joint’s bones in correct alignment. Difference: a tendon connects muscle to bone, whereas a ligament connects bone to bone.',
          explanation: 'This distinguishes two commonly confused connective tissue types by both their composition/structure and by their differing points of attachment.',
