          id: 'lifesci-g10-p1-2022-5-5',
          label: '5.5',
          topicId: 'life-sci-animal-tissues',
          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Distinguish between smooth muscle tissue and cardiac muscle tissue, referring to their structure, location and whether their contraction is voluntary or involuntary.',
          answer:
            'Smooth muscle tissue is composed of short, spindle-shaped, unstriated cells, each with a single nucleus; its contraction is involuntary, and it is found mainly in the walls of internal organs, such as the gut, blood vessels and bladder. Cardiac muscle tissue is composed of branched, striated cells joined end to end by intercalated discs, typically with a single nucleus per cell; its contraction is also involuntary, but it is found only in the walls of the heart.',
