          answer:
            'Water absorbed by root hair cells moves inward across the root cortex toward the xylem by osmosis, passing from cell to cell down a water potential gradient — some water moves through the cytoplasm of connected cells, while some moves instead through the porous cell walls and the spaces between cells, without necessarily entering the cytoplasm at all. At the endodermis, the innermost layer of the cortex surrounding the vascular tissue, water is directed through the membranes and cytoplasm of the endodermal cells before finally entering the xylem, ensuring that water passes through at least one living cell membrane on its way into the xylem.',
          explanation: 'This regulatory role of the endodermis helps the root control which substances, and how much water, ultimately reach the xylem for transport upward through the plant.',
