          answer:
            'As water evaporates from the mesophyll cells inside a leaf and diffuses out through the open stomata (transpiration), the mesophyll cells lose water and their water potential drops, so they draw in replacement water by osmosis from the xylem in the leaf veins. Because water molecules are strongly attracted to one another (cohesion, due to hydrogen bonding) and to the walls of the narrow xylem vessels (adhesion), this creates a continuous, unbroken pull that is transmitted like a chain all the way down the column of water filling the xylem, from the leaves through the stem to the roots, drawing further water up from the soil to replace what is lost — entirely as a passive, physical consequence of transpiration at the leaf surface, requiring no active pumping or energy expenditure by the plant itself.',
