          answer:
            'Because the strength of a bone depends heavily on the density and thickness of its bone tissue, particularly the network of trabeculae making up spongy bone and the thickness of the surrounding compact bone, unusually thin, porous spongy bone with reduced overall density would be considerably weaker and less able to withstand mechanical stress than bone of normal density. With a less dense, more porous internal structure, there is less solid bone material available at any cross-section of the bone to resist and distribute an applied force; under everyday mechanical stresses, such as the impact of walking or a minor fall, that a bone of normal density would easily withstand, this weakened bone is at much greater risk of cracking or breaking, since the applied force is more likely to exceed the reduced structural strength of the thinner, more porous bone tissue.',
