          topicId: 'life-sci-circulatory-system',
          grade: 10,
          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain why the left side of the heart is completely separated from the right side by a muscular wall (the septum), referring to the importance of keeping oxygenated and deoxygenated blood separate.',
          answer:
            'The right side of the heart receives and pumps deoxygenated blood returning from the body, on its way to the lungs, while the left side receives and pumps oxygenated blood returning from the lungs, on its way to the rest of the body. The septum completely separates these two sides, preventing oxygenated and deoxygenated blood from mixing within the heart; if they were allowed to mix, the blood pumped out to the body’s tissues via the aorta would carry a lower-than-normal concentration of oxygen, since it would be diluted with deoxygenated blood, which would reduce the efficiency of oxygen delivery to body tissues.',
          explanation: 'This complete separation, with a fully divided ventricle, is characteristic of the mammalian heart, allowing full double circulation with no mixing, unlike, for example, most reptiles, whose incompletely divided ventricle allows some mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood.',
