          answer:
            'A primary consumer is a herbivore that feeds directly on producers, occupying the second trophic level of a food chain — for example, a mopane worm (the caterpillar of the emperor moth) that feeds on the leaves of the mopane tree. A secondary consumer is a carnivore that feeds on primary consumers, occupying the third trophic level of the food chain — for example, a southern yellow-billed hornbill that preys on mopane worms.',
