          answer: 'The argument is wrong, and the reason is that floating ice is what allows the fish to survive rather than what kills them. Water molecules are held to one another by hydrogen bonds. In liquid water these bonds form, break and re-form, so the molecules can pack closely. As water cools towards freezing each molecule becomes fixed in hydrogen bonds to four others, which locks them into an open lattice that holds them further apart than they are in the liquid. The same mass therefore occupies a greater volume, so ice is less dense than liquid water and floats. The consequence for the pond is decisive. Ice forming at the surface floats and stays there, and because ice conducts heat poorly it insulates the water beneath, so the pond freezes downwards slowly and usually not to the bottom. The fish survive in liquid water below the ice, at around 4 degrees Celsius, which is where water is at its densest and so sinks and stays. If ice behaved like most other substances and were denser than its liquid, the ice would sink as it formed. Fresh water at the surface would then freeze in turn and sink too, and the pond would freeze solid from the bottom up, killing the fish and everything else in it -- and in a cold climate it might never fully thaw. So the learner has the outcome exactly backwards, and this is one of the properties on which life in cold fresh water depends.',
