          answer: 'As the soil dried out the cells lost more water by transpiration than they absorbed, so water left the vacuoles by osmosis, the vacuoles shrank and the cytoplasm and cell membrane no longer pressed outwards on the cell wall. The cells therefore lost their turgor pressure and became flaccid, and since the soft leaves and young stems depend on the outward push of turgid cells to keep them firm, the plant drooped. After watering, the soil water potential became higher than that of the root cells, so water was absorbed by osmosis, moved up the xylem and entered the cells, which swelled until the cell wall resisted further expansion and the cells became turgid again, restoring the firmness of the leaves and stems. Turgid parenchyma, assisted by living collenchyma in the stem, supplies most of the support in these young soft stems.',
