          answer: 'To raise the bag the biceps, which is the flexor on the front of the upper arm, contracts and shortens while the triceps, the extensor at the back, relaxes; the tendon of the biceps pulls on the radius, the forearm acts as a lever turning about the hinge joint at the elbow and the bag is lifted. To lower the bag the triceps contracts while the biceps relaxes, so the arm is straightened at the elbow. A single muscle could not do both because a muscle can only exert a force by contracting and pulling, never by pushing, so it cannot actively return a bone to its original position; a second muscle on the opposite side of the joint is needed to pull the bone back, and in doing so it stretches the first muscle again.',
