          answer: 'An artery has a thick wall containing a great deal of muscle and elastic tissue, a narrow lumen and no valves along its length, because it carries blood away from the heart at high pressure and the thick elastic wall must withstand and smooth out the surges of each heartbeat. A vein has a thin wall with much less muscle, a wide lumen and semilunar valves along its length, because it returns blood to the heart at low pressure, and the valves stop the slow-moving blood from flowing backwards when it is squeezed by the surrounding skeletal muscles.',
