          prompt: 'Whose position is better supported? Justify your answer, and explain how the ecologist could redraw the data so that the diagram has a normal pyramid shape.',
          answer: 'The ecologist\'s diagram is correct and the colleague has applied a true principle to the wrong quantity. Energy does decrease at each step up a food chain, because most of what one level takes in is lost as heat in respiration, in movement and in the material not eaten or not digested -- roughly a tenth is passed on. But a pyramid of numbers counts individuals, and an individual is not a fixed amount of energy. One oak is a single organism holding an enormous amount of living material, and it can support thousands of very small caterpillars from it, so the count at the producer level is one while the count above it is in the thousands, and the shape is inverted at the base. Nothing about that contradicts the energy rule: the energy in the tree is still far greater than the energy in all the caterpillars. What produces the shape is the mismatch in body size, which counting ignores. To redraw it with a normal shape the ecologist should draw a pyramid of biomass instead, measuring the dry mass of organisms at each level rather than their number. The mass of the single oak is far greater than the combined mass of the caterpillars feeding on it, which is greater in turn than the mass of the birds feeding on them, so the diagram narrows properly at each step. A pyramid of energy, measuring the energy at each level over a period of time, is better still and is never inverted.',
