          marks: 4,
          context: 'A large area of indigenous forest on a mountain slope is cleared for building.',
          prompt: 'Explain how the removal of the trees affects the water cycle in that area, referring to at least THREE of its processes.',
          answer: 'Transpiration falls sharply because the leaves that returned water vapour to the atmosphere have been removed, so less water vapour rises from the slope, less condensation takes place above it and local rainfall may decrease. Infiltration into the soil also decreases because there are no longer roots and leaf litter to hold the soil open and to slow the water down, so less water soaks in to recharge the groundwater. Consequently surface run-off increases, and rain now flows quickly down the bare slope, carrying topsoil with it, which causes erosion and flooding of the rivers below while leaving the slope drier between rains.',
