          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 4,
          context: 'Cells taken from the green leaf of an onion plant are compared with cells taken from the white storage leaves of the same onion bulb.',
          prompt: 'Explain why the leaf cells contain chloroplasts while the bulb cells do not, and name and give the function of the plastid found in the bulb cells.',
          answer: 'The leaf cells are exposed to light above the ground, so they contain chloroplasts holding the green pigment chlorophyll that traps light energy for photosynthesis. The cells of the bulb are underground and receive no light, so chlorophyll would be of no use to them and no chloroplasts develop; instead they contain leucoplasts, which are colourless plastids that store the food, mainly as starch, that was made in the leaves and translocated to the bulb.',
          explanation: 'Plastids are a family of related organelles whose type reflects the function of the cell and its exposure to light, which is why the same plant contains both chloroplasts and leucoplasts.',
