          context: 'Long fibres taken from the stems of the flax plant are woven into linen cloth and rope.',
          prompt: 'Name the plant tissue from which these fibres come, describe where it is found in the stem, and explain TWO properties of the tissue that make it suitable for making rope.',
          answer: 'The fibres come from sclerenchyma tissue, which occurs in strands or caps at the outer edge of the vascular bundles and in bands in the cortex of the stem. Sclerenchyma fibres are suitable for rope because their walls are heavily thickened with lignin, which makes them extremely strong and able to resist a large pulling force without breaking, and because the cells are long, narrow and tapered and overlap one another, so they can be spun into a continuous thread; being dead and lignified they also resist decay and do not rot or shrink easily.',
