          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Explain TWO ways in which palisade mesophyll differs from spongy mesophyll in a dicotyledonous leaf, and relate each difference to the function of that layer.',
          answer: 'Palisade mesophyll cells are elongated and packed closely together in one or two layers immediately below the upper epidermis and contain many more chloroplasts, which allows them to absorb the maximum amount of the light falling on the leaf and makes them the main site of photosynthesis. Spongy mesophyll cells are irregular in shape, contain fewer chloroplasts and are loosely arranged with large intercellular air spaces between them, which allows carbon dioxide to diffuse freely from the stomata to all the photosynthesising cells and allows oxygen and water vapour to diffuse out.',
          explanation: 'The leaf divides the work of photosynthesis between a layer specialised for light absorption near the surface and a layer specialised for gas exchange near the stomata.',
