          cognitiveLevel: 3,
          marks: 5,
          context: 'A gardener cuts off the growing tip of every shoot of a young hedge. Within a few weeks the hedge becomes much bushier, with many new side shoots.',
          prompt: 'Explain this result with reference to the meristematic tissue in the shoot tip and in the side buds, and predict what would happen to the height of the hedge if the gardener never pruned it.',
          answer: 'The tip of each shoot contains an apical meristem whose cells divide by mitosis and then elongate, which makes the shoot grow longer, and while the apical meristem is present it suppresses the growth of the lateral buds lower down the stem. When the gardener removes the shoot tips, this suppression is removed, so the meristematic tissue in the lateral buds along the stem becomes active and begins dividing, and each of those buds grows out into a new side shoot, which makes the hedge dense and bushy. If the gardener never pruned the hedge, the apical meristems would continue to divide and the shoots would keep growing upwards, so the hedge would become tall and leggy with long bare stems and few side branches.',
          explanation: 'Pruning is an everyday application of the difference between apical and lateral meristems, since removing the dominant apical meristem redirects growth into the buds it was holding back.',
