          cognitiveLevel: 2,
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Compare meristematic tissue with mature permanent tissue in respect of cell wall thickness, vacuole size, relative nucleus size, intercellular spaces and the ability to divide.',
          answer: 'Meristematic cells have thin walls made only of cellulose, while permanent cells often have thickened walls that may contain extra cellulose or lignin. Meristematic cells have no vacuole or only tiny vacuoles, while mature permanent cells usually have one large central vacuole. The nucleus of a meristematic cell is large in relation to the small volume of the cell, while in a permanent cell the nucleus is small in relation to the cell, or may be absent altogether as in a mature sieve tube or xylem vessel. Meristematic cells are packed tightly together with no intercellular air spaces, while permanent tissues such as spongy mesophyll and cortex have distinct air spaces between the cells. Meristematic cells divide continually by mitosis, while permanent cells have differentiated and normally no longer divide.',
          explanation: 'Every one of these differences follows from the fact that a meristematic cell is built for rapid division and growth, whereas a permanent cell has already specialised for a fixed function.',
