          marks: 4,
          prompt: 'Distinguish between a scavenger and a decomposer in the way each obtains its food, give ONE South African example of each, and state which of the two returns mineral salts to the soil.',
          answer: 'A scavenger is an animal that feeds on the remains of dead animals it did not kill, taking the food into its body and digesting it internally, for example a hyena or a vulture. A decomposer is an organism such as a bacterium or fungus that secretes digestive enzymes onto dead organic matter and then absorbs the simple soluble products, digesting the food outside its body, for example a mushroom or soil bacteria. It is the decomposers that break the organic compounds down completely and release mineral salts such as nitrates and phosphates back into the soil.',
          explanation: 'Scavengers break large remains into smaller pieces and so speed decomposition up, but only decomposers complete the breakdown to inorganic form and so close the nutrient cycles.',
