          answer: 'The process is decomposition, also called putrefaction or ammonification, carried out by decomposing bacteria and fungi. The ammonium compounds released are then acted on by nitrifying bacteria, which oxidise them first to nitrites and then to nitrates. A plant cannot use the nitrogen in a dead leaf directly because it is locked up in large, insoluble organic molecules such as proteins and nucleic acids, and roots can only absorb nitrogen in the form of soluble inorganic ions, mainly nitrates, so the organic nitrogen must first be broken down and converted.',
