          marks: 6,
          context: 'Identical mesh bags of dried leaves were buried in three places and the percentage of the leaf mass lost after six months was recorded. Coastal forest in KwaZulu-Natal (warm, mean rainfall 1 000 mm per year): 78% lost. Highveld grassland (cool, 600 mm): 41% lost. Northern Cape desert (hot, 90 mm): 9% lost.',
          prompt: 'Identify the two abiotic factors that differ between the sites, explain how each affects the rate at which the leaves break down, and explain what the results mean for the depth of the litter layer and the speed of nutrient cycling at each site.',
          answer: 'The two abiotic factors are temperature and rainfall, and therefore soil moisture. Higher temperatures speed up decomposition because the enzymes secreted by decomposing bacteria and fungi work faster as the temperature rises towards their optimum, so the decomposers grow and reproduce more rapidly; this is why the cool Highveld lost less mass than the warm coastal forest. Adequate moisture is essential because the decomposers need water in which to secrete their enzymes and absorb the soluble products, and because their own cells dry out without it, which explains why the hot but very dry desert site lost only 9%, showing that heat alone is not enough. At the coastal forest site the litter is broken down almost as fast as it falls, so the litter layer stays thin and nutrients are released and reabsorbed by the plants very quickly, giving rapid nutrient cycling. In the grassland decomposition is slower, so litter and humus accumulate and the cycle turns more slowly. In the desert the dead material breaks down so slowly that leaves and dead stems can lie on the surface for years, nutrients stay locked in the dead tissue and nutrient cycling is extremely slow, which is one reason why desert soils support so little plant growth even when they are watered.',
